window.TREE={"subject":"Area III · Select Transactions","key":"far_area3_transactions","brand":"Terra Prep","area":"III","law_asof":"2026-09-29","parts":[{"no":1,"name":"Area III · Select Transactions","chs":["1-01","1-02","1-03","1-04","1-05","1-06","1-07"],"color":"--fam-eth"}],"chs":[{"key":"1-01","part":1,"no":1,"name":"Accounting Changes and Error Corrections","grp":"A","nodes":[1]},{"key":"1-02","part":1,"no":2,"name":"Contingencies and Commitments","grp":"B","nodes":[2]},{"key":"1-03","part":1,"no":3,"name":"Revenue Recognition","grp":"C","nodes":[3]},{"key":"1-04","part":1,"no":4,"name":"Income Taxes","grp":"D","nodes":[4]},{"key":"1-05","part":1,"no":5,"name":"Fair Value Measurement","grp":"E","nodes":[5]},{"key":"1-06","part":1,"no":6,"name":"Leases","grp":"F","nodes":[6]},{"key":"1-07","part":1,"no":7,"name":"Subsequent Events","grp":"G","nodes":[7]}],"nodes":[{"no":1,"part":1,"ch":1,"title":"Accounting Changes and Error Corrections","name":"Accounting Changes and Error Corrections","vol":22,"q":22,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"A"},{"no":2,"part":1,"ch":2,"title":"Contingencies and Commitments","name":"Contingencies and Commitments","vol":18,"q":18,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"B"},{"no":3,"part":1,"ch":3,"title":"Revenue Recognition","name":"Revenue Recognition","vol":32,"q":32,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"C"},{"no":4,"part":1,"ch":4,"title":"Income Taxes","name":"Income Taxes","vol":28,"q":28,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"D"},{"no":5,"part":1,"ch":5,"title":"Fair Value Measurement","name":"Fair Value Measurement","vol":14,"q":14,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"E"},{"no":6,"part":1,"ch":6,"title":"Leases","name":"Leases","vol":26,"q":26,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"F"},{"no":7,"part":1,"ch":7,"title":"Subsequent Events","name":"Subsequent Events","vol":10,"q":10,"cs":0,"law":0,"grp":"G"}],"stat":{"parts":1,"chs":7,"nodes":7,"q":150}};
window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"FAR-III-A-0001","t":"Which of the following is a change in accounting principle rather than a change in estimate or the correction of an error?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is a change in accounting principle rather than a change in estimate or the correction of an error?","d":"","o":["A company shortens the expected useful life of its delivery trucks after reviewing their actual usage","A company that recognized revenue only when cash was received begins recognizing it when earned","A company adopts straight-line depreciation for a newly purchased class of assets that it did not own before","A company switches its inventory cost-flow assumption from FIFO to weighted-average cost because the new method is preferable"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-20; ASC 250-10-45-2","rat":"Moving from one generally accepted method to another is a change in accounting principle and must be justified as preferable.","ex":["A revised useful life is a change in accounting estimate. It arises from new information about future benefits, not from choosing a different principle.","Cash-basis revenue recognition was never generally accepted. Moving to GAAP is the correction of an error, not a change in principle.","Adopting a method for a new type of transaction or event is not a change in principle because no prior method existed for that item.","Moving from one generally accepted method to another is a change in accounting principle and must be justified as preferable."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in principle is a switch between two acceptable methods and requires a preferability justification.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0002","t":"A company voluntarily changes an accounting principle and the change is permitted because the new principle is preferable. How does it report the change in its comparative financial statements?","mc":{"s":"A company voluntarily changes an accounting principle and the change is permitted because the new principle is preferable. How does it report the change in its comparative financial statements?","d":"","o":["It applies the new principle only to current and future periods and leaves the prior statements unchanged","It reports the cumulative effect as a separate line in the current-year income statement","It reports the change only in the notes, with no adjustment to any reported amount","It applies the new principle retrospectively, adjusting the opening balance of retained earnings of the earliest period presented"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-5; ASC 250-10-45-7","rat":"Retrospective application recasts prior periods presented as if the new principle had always been used, with the cumulative effect of earlier periods in opening retained earnings.","ex":["Prospective application is used for changes in estimate, or when retrospective application is impracticable. It is not the default for a change in principle.","A cumulative-effect line in current income was the old treatment. Current guidance places the effect in opening retained earnings of the earliest period shown.","Disclosure alone is not enough. Prior-period amounts are adjusted, and the notes explain the nature of, and reason for, the change.","Retrospective application recasts prior periods presented as if the new principle had always been used, with the cumulative effect of earlier periods in opening retained earnings."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Retrospective application is the default for voluntary changes in principle, with the cumulative effect in opening retained earnings.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0003","t":"Sequoia Freight Inc. bought equipment for $120,000 on January 1, 2023, with an estimated 10-year life and no salvage value, and uses straight-line depreciation. On January 1, 2026, after a review, management concluded that the total life is 8 years from acquisition. What is the depreciation expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Sequoia Freight Inc. bought equipment for $120,000 on January 1, 2023, with an estimated 10-year life and no salvage value, and uses straight-line depreciation. On January 1, 2026, after a review, management concluded that the total life is 8 years from acquisition. What is the depreciation expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$10,500","$12,000","$15,000","$16,800"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-17","rat":"Carrying amount $84,000 after three years of $12,000 is spread over the 5 remaining years: $16,800.","ex":["Spreads the carrying amount over the new total life of 8 years instead of the 5 remaining years.","Keeps the old $12,000 charge. The revised life must be used from the date of the change.","Divides the original cost by the new total life. A change in estimate is applied to the carrying amount over the remaining life, not to original cost.","Carrying amount $84,000 after three years of $12,000 is spread over the 5 remaining years: $16,800."],"wt":["calc","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in estimate is applied prospectively: remaining carrying amount less salvage over the remaining revised life.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Annual depreciation before change 120,000 / 10 = 12,000","Accumulated depreciation 12,000 x 3 = 36,000; carrying amount 84,000","Remaining life 8 - 3 = 5 years; 84,000 / 5 = 16,800"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0004","t":"Harbor Point Corp. acquired machinery on January 1, 2023, for $250,000. It uses straight-line depreciation over an 8-year life with a salvage value of $10,000. On January 1, 2026, management revised the total useful life to 10 years and the salvage value to $20,000. What is 2026 depreciation expense?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Point Corp. acquired machinery on January 1, 2023, for $250,000. It uses straight-line depreciation over an 8-year life with a salvage value of $10,000. On January 1, 2026, management revised the total useful life to 10 years and the salvage value to $20,000. What is 2026 depreciation expense?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$21,429","$22,857","$23,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-17","rat":"Carrying amount $160,000 less revised salvage $20,000 = $140,000, spread over 7 remaining years.","ex":["Carrying amount $160,000 less revised salvage $20,000 = $140,000, spread over 7 remaining years.","Uses the old salvage value. The revised salvage estimate applies from the date of the change.","Ignores salvage value entirely. Depreciable base is carrying amount less expected salvage.","Applies the new life and salvage to original cost, ignoring the three years already depreciated."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Revised depreciation equals carrying amount less revised salvage, divided by the remaining life, from the change date.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Accumulated depreciation to 12/31/25: (250,000 - 10,000) / 8 x 3 = 90,000","Carrying amount 160,000; less revised salvage 20,000 = 140,000","Remaining life 10 - 3 = 7; 140,000 / 7 = 20,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0005","t":"Delmar Foods Inc. voluntarily changes from LIFO to FIFO in 2026 and applies the change retrospectively. Inventory at December 31, 2025, was $560,000 under LIFO and $640,000 under FIFO. The tax rate is 25%. What is the increase in January 1, 2026, retained earnings reported for this change?","mc":{"s":"Delmar Foods Inc. voluntarily changes from LIFO to FIFO in 2026 and applies the change retrospectively. Inventory at December 31, 2025, was $560,000 under LIFO and $640,000 under FIFO. The tax rate is 25%. What is the increase in January 1, 2026, retained earnings reported for this change?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$60,000","$80,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-5","rat":"The $80,000 higher FIFO inventory raises prior cumulative income by $80,000, less 25% tax = $60,000.","ex":["Reports only the tax effect. The adjustment is the pretax difference reduced by tax, not the tax itself.","The $80,000 higher FIFO inventory raises prior cumulative income by $80,000, less 25% tax = $60,000.","Stops at the pretax amount. Retained earnings is adjusted net of the related income tax effect.","Adds tax instead of deducting it. Higher income creates higher tax expense, which reduces the net adjustment."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The cumulative effect of a change in principle is adjusted to opening retained earnings, net of tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Inventory difference 640,000 - 560,000 = 80,000 (higher prior cumulative income)","Tax effect 80,000 x 25% = 20,000","Net increase in retained earnings 80,000 - 20,000 = 60,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0006","t":"On January 1, 2025, Quill Systems Inc. paid $90,000 for equipment with a 5-year life and no salvage value, and mistakenly recorded it as repair expense. The error was found in 2026, and the 2025 statements are already issued. The tax rate is 20%. What is the net-of-tax adjustment to the opening retained earnings of 2026, assuming straight-line depreciation would have been used?","mc":{"s":"On January 1, 2025, Quill Systems Inc. paid $90,000 for equipment with a 5-year life and no salvage value, and mistakenly recorded it as repair expense. The error was found in 2026, and the 2025 statements are already issued. The tax rate is 20%. What is the net-of-tax adjustment to the opening retained earnings of 2026, assuming straight-line depreciation would have been used?","d":"","o":["$14,400","$57,600","$72,000","$90,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"Net asset that should be on hand at 12/31/25 is $90,000 - $18,000 = $72,000; after 20% tax the retained earnings adjustment is $57,600.","ex":["Reflects only the after-tax depreciation. The expensed cost of the asset is also reversed.","Net asset that should be on hand at 12/31/25 is $90,000 - $18,000 = $72,000; after 20% tax the retained earnings adjustment is $57,600.","Omits the tax effect. The correction to retained earnings is presented net of income tax.","Reverses the full $90,000 with no depreciation and no tax. The asset would have been depreciated for 2025 and the effect taxed."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A material prior-period error is corrected by restating, with the adjustment to opening retained earnings shown net of tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Depreciation for 2025 90,000 / 5 = 18,000","Pretax overstatement of prior expense 90,000 - 18,000 = 72,000","Net of 20% tax: 72,000 x 0.80 = 57,600"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0007","t":"Tamarack Retail Inc. reported 2026 net income of $400,000. It later found that ending inventory was overstated by $30,000 at December 31, 2025, and understated by $10,000 at December 31, 2026. Ignoring income taxes, what is the correct 2026 net income?","mc":{"s":"Tamarack Retail Inc. reported 2026 net income of $400,000. It later found that ending inventory was overstated by $30,000 at December 31, 2025, and understated by $10,000 at December 31, 2026. Ignoring income taxes, what is the correct 2026 net income?","d":"","o":["$360,000","$380,000","$420,000","$440,000"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23; ASC 330-10-30-1","rat":"Overstated opening inventory raised 2026 cost of goods sold, so income was understated by $30,000.","ex":["Reverses both signs. Opening overstatement lowers income of the following year, so the correction is an increase.","Subtracts the opening error and adds the ending error, the reverse of the true effect on each.","Treats the understated ending inventory as if it overstated income. Understated ending inventory overstates cost of goods sold and lowers income.","Overstated opening inventory raised 2026 cost of goods sold, so income was understated by $30,000. Understated ending inventory raised it again by $10,000. Corrected income is $440,000."],"wt":["calc","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Opening inventory errors affect income opposite to the prior year; ending inventory errors affect income in the same direction as inventory.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Overstated opening inventory 30,000 -> 2026 COGS too high -> income understated by 30,000","Understated ending inventory 10,000 -> 2026 COGS too high -> income understated by 10,000","Corrected income 400,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 = 440,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0008","t":"Wexford Services Inc. did not accrue wages at year-end. Unrecorded wages payable were $12,000 at December 31, 2025, and $18,000 at December 31, 2026, and all wages were expensed when paid. Reported 2026 income before tax was $250,000. What is the corrected 2026 income before tax?","mc":{"s":"Wexford Services Inc. did not accrue wages at year-end. Unrecorded wages payable were $12,000 at December 31, 2025, and $18,000 at December 31, 2026, and all wages were expensed when paid. Reported 2026 income before tax was $250,000. What is the corrected 2026 income before tax?","d":"","o":["$220,000","$244,000","$256,000","$268,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"The 2025 wages paid in 2026 were wrongly expensed in 2026 (+$12,000), and the 2026 wages unpaid at year-end were omitted (-$18,000): $244,000.","ex":["Deducts both amounts. Only the current year-end accrual reduces income; the prior accrual was paid in 2026 and belongs to 2025.","The 2025 wages paid in 2026 were wrongly expensed in 2026 (+$12,000), and the 2026 wages unpaid at year-end were omitted (-$18,000): $244,000.","Reverses both adjustments. The prior-year accrual raises 2026 income when removed; the year-end accrual lowers it.","Adds the current year-end accrual instead of deducting it. Unrecorded expense means income was overstated."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corrected expense equals expense paid, less opening accrual, plus closing accrual; an omitted accrual overstates income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["2026 expense as reported = cash paid for wages","Correct 2026 expense = paid - 12,000 (belongs to 2025) + 18,000 (accrue 2026)","Income correction = +12,000 - 18,000 = -6,000; 250,000 - 6,000 = 244,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0009","t":"In 2026, a company discovers that an error in the 2025 financial statements, which have been issued, was material. Which treatment is required?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, a company discovers that an error in the 2025 financial statements, which have been issued, was material. Which treatment is required?","d":"","o":["Include the correction in 2026 net income as an extraordinary item","Treat it as a change in estimate and adjust only current and future periods","Disclose the error in the notes only, leaving 2025 amounts unchanged","Restate the prior-period statements and adjust opening retained earnings of the earliest period presented"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23; ASC 250-10-50-7","rat":"Material errors are corrected by restatement of previously issued statements, with disclosure of the nature of the error and its effect.","ex":["Errors are never corrected through current income, and extraordinary items no longer exist in US GAAP.","An error results from misuse of facts or a mistake, not from new information. It is not a change in estimate.","Disclosure without correcting the affected amounts does not satisfy the restatement requirement.","Material errors are corrected by restatement of previously issued statements, with disclosure of the nature of the error and its effect."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Material errors in issued statements are corrected by restating prior periods and disclosing the error's effects.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0010","t":"A parent company changes its reporting entity by adding a previously unconsolidated subsidiary to its consolidated financial statements, because it now controls the subsidiary through common ownership changes among affiliates. How is the change reported?","mc":{"s":"A parent company changes its reporting entity by adding a previously unconsolidated subsidiary to its consolidated financial statements, because it now controls the subsidiary through common ownership changes among affiliates. How is the change reported?","d":"","o":["Prospectively from the date of the change, with no restatement of prior periods","As a correction of an error in every prior period presented","As a component of income from discontinued operations in the current period","Retrospectively, by recasting the financial statements of all prior periods presented as though the new entity had been included from the start"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-21","rat":"A change in reporting entity is a special type of change in principle and is applied retrospectively to all periods presented.","ex":["Prospective treatment is reserved for changes in estimate. A change in reporting entity requires recasting.","The prior statements were not wrong when issued. A change in reporting entity is not an error correction.","Discontinued operations relate to disposals. Adding an entity does not create a discontinued operation.","A change in reporting entity is a special type of change in principle and is applied retrospectively to all periods presented."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in reporting entity is applied retrospectively by recasting all prior periods presented.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0011","t":"Orchid Supply Co. had been estimating uncollectible accounts at 4% of ending receivables. At December 31, 2026, management revised the estimate to 6% based on new customer credit data. Ending accounts receivable are $800,000, and the allowance has a credit balance of $10,000 before adjustment. What is bad debt expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Orchid Supply Co. had been estimating uncollectible accounts at 4% of ending receivables. At December 31, 2026, management revised the estimate to 6% based on new customer credit data. Ending accounts receivable are $800,000, and the allowance has a credit balance of $10,000 before adjustment. What is bad debt expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$22,000","$38,000","$48,000","$58,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-17; ASC 326-20-30-1","rat":"Required allowance $48,000 less existing credit balance $10,000 = $38,000.","ex":["Uses the old 4% rate. The revised rate applies as of the date of the new information.","Required allowance $48,000 less existing credit balance $10,000 = $38,000. The change in estimate flows through current expense.","Ignores the existing $10,000 credit balance. Expense equals the required ending allowance less the balance already carried.","Adds the existing balance to the required allowance instead of netting it."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A revised uncollectible estimate is recognized in current expense; the allowance is brought to the newly estimated balance.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Required allowance 800,000 x 6% = 48,000","Less existing credit balance 10,000","Bad debt expense 38,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0012","t":"In 2025, Ironbridge Tech Inc. recognized $50,000 of revenue for services that were actually performed in 2026. The 2025 statements were issued, the error is material, and the tax rate is 30%. By how much is beginning retained earnings for 2026 reduced when the 2025 statements are restated?","mc":{"s":"In 2025, Ironbridge Tech Inc. recognized $50,000 of revenue for services that were actually performed in 2026. The 2025 statements were issued, the error is material, and the tax rate is 30%. By how much is beginning retained earnings for 2026 reduced when the 2025 statements are restated?","d":"","o":["$15,000","$35,000","$50,000","$65,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"The 2025 revenue overstatement of $50,000 less the $15,000 tax effect is $35,000 lower cumulative net income at the start of 2026.","ex":["Only the tax portion. The retained earnings effect is the net income overstatement after tax.","The 2025 revenue overstatement of $50,000 less the $15,000 tax effect is $35,000 lower cumulative net income at the start of 2026.","Pretax amount; the adjustment to retained earnings is net of tax.","Adds the tax to the revenue error. Lower revenue also lowers income tax, so the net effect is smaller than $50,000."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A prior-period error's effect on retained earnings is stated net of the income tax effect.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Overstated 2025 revenue 50,000 with no offsetting cost","Tax effect 50,000 x 30% = 15,000","Reduction of beginning retained earnings 50,000 - 15,000 = 35,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0013","t":"Larch Hardware Inc. changes from LIFO to FIFO in 2026. FIFO exceeded LIFO inventory by $60,000 at December 31, 2024, and by $100,000 at December 31, 2025. The 2025 net income originally reported was $210,000, and the tax rate is 25%. In the 2026 comparative statements, what is 2025 net income after retrospective application?","mc":{"s":"Larch Hardware Inc. changes from LIFO to FIFO in 2026. FIFO exceeded LIFO inventory by $60,000 at December 31, 2024, and by $100,000 at December 31, 2025. The 2025 net income originally reported was $210,000, and the tax rate is 25%. In the 2026 comparative statements, what is 2025 net income after retrospective application?","d":"","o":["$240,000","$250,000","$255,000","$285,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-5; ASC 330-10-30-9","rat":"The FIFO inventory rose by $40,000 during 2025, lowering 2025 cost of goods sold by $40,000.","ex":["The FIFO inventory rose by $40,000 during 2025, lowering 2025 cost of goods sold by $40,000. After 25% tax, income rises $30,000 to $240,000.","Adds the pretax amount. Restated income reflects the tax effect of the higher pretax income.","Uses the beginning-of-year difference instead of the change during 2025.","Uses the full year-end 2025 difference, which includes the cumulative amount from 2024. Only the change during 2025 affects 2025 income."],"wt":["","trap","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Restated income for a period equals reported income plus the after-tax change in the FIFO-LIFO inventory difference during that period.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Change in FIFO-LIFO difference during 2025 = 100,000 - 60,000 = 40,000","After tax at 25%: 40,000 x 0.75 = 30,000","Restated 2025 income 210,000 + 30,000 = 240,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0014","t":"When may a company voluntarily change from one generally accepted accounting principle to another?","mc":{"s":"When may a company voluntarily change from one generally accepted accounting principle to another?","d":"","o":["The change lowers reported income tax expense for the period","The change is immaterial to the current year's income, whatever the reason for making it or the effect on future periods","The company has the approval of its lender","The company can justify that the new principle is preferable and it discloses the reason"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-2; ASC 250-10-50-1","rat":"A voluntary change is allowed only if the entity justifies that the alternative principle is preferable.","ex":["Tax savings are not a reason for a voluntary change. Preferability is judged on the quality of financial reporting.","Immateriality of the current-year effect does not by itself justify a change in principle.","Lender approval is a contractual matter. GAAP requires a preferability justification.","A voluntary change is allowed only if the entity justifies that the alternative principle is preferable."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A voluntary change in principle is allowed only when the entity justifies the new principle as preferable.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0015","t":"Beacon Outfitters Inc. understated its ending inventory by $20,000 at December 31, 2025, and never discovered the error. Which describes the effect at December 31, 2026, ignoring taxes?","mc":{"s":"Beacon Outfitters Inc. understated its ending inventory by $20,000 at December 31, 2025, and never discovered the error. Which describes the effect at December 31, 2026, ignoring taxes?","d":"","o":["Retained earnings is understated by $20,000 because the 2025 error was not corrected in the accounts","Retained earnings is overstated by $20,000 because 2026 income is overstated","Ending 2026 inventory is understated by $20,000 as a result of the error","Retained earnings at December 31, 2026, is correct because the error reversed in 2026"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"The 2025 understatement reduced 2025 income; the same amount overstated 2026 income through opening inventory, so retained earnings at the end of 2026 is correct.","ex":["Ignores the reversal. Opening inventory in 2026 is too low, which lowers 2026 cost of goods sold and offsets the 2025 effect.","The 2026 overstatement exactly offsets the 2025 understatement, so cumulative retained earnings is not misstated.","The 2025 error affects opening 2026 inventory only. Ending 2026 inventory is measured from the count at that date and is unaffected.","The 2025 understatement reduced 2025 income; the same amount overstated 2026 income through opening inventory, so retained earnings at the end of 2026 is correct."],"wt":["trap","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Ending inventory errors are counterbalancing: they misstate income in two years but retained earnings is correct after the second year.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0016","t":"Juniper Biosciences Inc. acquired a patent on January 1, 2024, for $480,000 and amortized it straight-line over 12 years. On January 1, 2026, it decided the patent's total useful life from acquisition is 8 years. What is 2026 amortization expense?","mc":{"s":"Juniper Biosciences Inc. acquired a patent on January 1, 2024, for $480,000 and amortized it straight-line over 12 years. On January 1, 2026, it decided the patent's total useful life from acquisition is 8 years. What is 2026 amortization expense?","d":"","o":["$40,000","$60,000","$66,667","$80,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-17; ASC 350-30-35-9","rat":"Carrying amount $400,000 is amortized over the 6 remaining years: $66,667.","ex":["Retains the old charge of $40,000. The revised life applies from the date of change.","Divides the original cost by the new total life. The change in estimate applies to the remaining carrying amount.","Carrying amount $400,000 is amortized over the 6 remaining years: $66,667.","Amortizes over 5 years, an arbitrary shortening. The remaining period is the new total life less the years already elapsed."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in amortization period is prospective: carrying amount divided by the revised remaining life.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amortization before change 480,000 / 12 = 40,000","Accumulated amortization 40,000 x 2 = 80,000; carrying amount 400,000","Remaining life 8 - 2 = 6; 400,000 / 6 = 66,667"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0017","t":"Granite Ridge Inc. bought equipment on January 1, 2023, for $260,000 (10-year life, no salvage) and used straight-line depreciation. On January 1, 2026, it changes to double-declining balance, which is preferable, and keeps the original 10-year total life, so 7 years remain at the change date. What is depreciation expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Granite Ridge Inc. bought equipment on January 1, 2023, for $260,000 (10-year life, no salvage) and used straight-line depreciation. On January 1, 2026, it changes to double-declining balance, which is preferable, and keeps the original 10-year total life, so 7 years remain at the change date. What is depreciation expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$26,000","$36,400","$52,000","$75,504"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-18; ASC 250-10-45-19","rat":"A change in depreciation method is a change in estimate effected by a change in principle, applied prospectively: carrying amount $182,000 x 2/7 = $52,000.","ex":["Continues straight-line. The new method is applied from the date of the change.","Applies the DDB rate of 20% (based on the original 10-year life) to the carrying amount. The rate must reflect the 7 remaining years.","A change in depreciation method is a change in estimate effected by a change in principle, applied prospectively: carrying amount $182,000 x 2/7 = $52,000.","Books a catch-up as if DDB had always been used and then adds the fourth-year DDB charge. A change in depreciation method is not applied retrospectively."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A depreciation method change is treated as a change in estimate effected by a change in principle, applied prospectively.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Carrying amount at 1/1/26: 260,000 - 26,000 x 3 = 182,000","Remaining life 7 years; DDB rate 2/7","Depreciation 182,000 x 2/7 = 52,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0018","t":"Cypress Motors Inc. previously reported retained earnings of $900,000 at January 1, 2026. In 2026 it discovers a material 2025 error whose correction, net of tax, reduces opening retained earnings by $60,000. Net income for 2026 is $300,000, and dividends declared are $100,000. What is retained earnings at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"Cypress Motors Inc. previously reported retained earnings of $900,000 at January 1, 2026. In 2026 it discovers a material 2025 error whose correction, net of tax, reduces opening retained earnings by $60,000. Net income for 2026 is $300,000, and dividends declared are $100,000. What is retained earnings at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$980,000","$1,040,000","$1,100,000","$1,160,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"$900,000 - $60,000 restated opening = $840,000; + $300,000 income - $100,000 dividends = $1,040,000.","ex":["Deducts the adjustment twice.","$900,000 - $60,000 restated opening = $840,000; + $300,000 income - $100,000 dividends = $1,040,000.","Omits the prior-period adjustment. The restated opening balance is the starting point.","Adds the error correction instead of subtracting it. The correction reduces opening retained earnings."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Restated opening retained earnings plus net income less dividends gives the ending balance.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Restated opening retained earnings 900,000 - 60,000 = 840,000","Add net income 300,000 = 1,140,000","Less dividends 100,000 = 1,040,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0019","t":"Meridian Metals Inc. changes its inventory method from FIFO to LIFO in 2026. Records needed to compute the effect on prior years' LIFO layers are unavailable, so retrospective application is impracticable. Which is the appropriate treatment?","mc":{"s":"Meridian Metals Inc. changes its inventory method from FIFO to LIFO in 2026. Records needed to compute the effect on prior years' LIFO layers are unavailable, so retrospective application is impracticable. Which is the appropriate treatment?","d":"","o":["Restate all prior years by estimating hypothetical LIFO layers from industry price indexes","Reject the change because a change to LIFO cannot be made without retrospective restatement","Report the cumulative difference between FIFO and LIFO as a gain or loss in 2026 income","Use the carrying amount of inventory at the start of 2026 as the opening LIFO base layer and apply LIFO prospectively from that date"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-9; ASC 250-10-45-6","rat":"When it is impracticable to determine prior-period effects, the change to LIFO is applied prospectively with opening inventory becoming the base layer.","ex":["Retrospective application is required only when practicable. Where the needed data cannot be obtained, prospective application is used.","The standard explicitly allows prospective application when retrospective application is impracticable.","Cumulative effects of a change in principle are not recognized in current-period income.","When it is impracticable to determine prior-period effects, the change to LIFO is applied prospectively with opening inventory becoming the base layer."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If prior-period effects are impracticable to determine, the new principle applies prospectively from the earliest practicable date.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0020","t":"Which of the following is an error, requiring correction of previously issued financial statements, rather than a change in estimate?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is an error, requiring correction of previously issued financial statements, rather than a change in estimate?","d":"","o":["A company revises its warranty accrual rate after receiving a higher claim rate on new models","A company sells its factory and recognizes a gain on the sale in the year of sale","A company lowers its allowance for credit losses after collecting more receivables than expected","A company discovers that it omitted a major supplier invoice from year-end accounts payable because of a filing mistake"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-20","rat":"Overlooking available information and mistakes in applying GAAP are errors, which are corrected by restatement when material.","ex":["New information about future claims changes an estimate. It is applied prospectively.","A gain on sale is an ordinary transaction and is not an error.","Updating an estimate for actual experience is a change in estimate, not an error.","Overlooking available information and mistakes in applying GAAP are errors, which are corrected by restatement when material."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An error stems from oversight or misapplication of GAAP, while a change in estimate stems from new information.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0021","t":"During its 2026 audit, Pine Valley Corp. found two matters. First, depreciation on a building placed in service in 2024 was never recorded because the asset was left in construction in progress. Second, management now expects a machine to last 2 years longer than originally estimated. Which treatment is correct?","mc":{"s":"During its 2026 audit, Pine Valley Corp. found two matters. First, depreciation on a building placed in service in 2024 was never recorded because the asset was left in construction in progress. Second, management now expects a machine to last 2 years longer than originally estimated. Which treatment is correct?","d":"","o":["Apply both matters prospectively because both affect future depreciation","Restate prior periods for both matters","Record the omitted depreciation in 2026 income and restate for the machine","Restate prior periods for the omitted depreciation; apply the machine's longer life prospectively"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-45-17; ASC 250-10-45-23","rat":"Omitted depreciation is an error and is corrected by restatement.","ex":["Only the estimate change is prospective. The omitted depreciation is an error in prior periods.","A change in estimate is not restated. Only the error is corrected in prior statements.","The reverse of the correct treatment. Errors are corrected in the prior periods, and estimates are not restated.","Omitted depreciation is an error and is corrected by restatement. The revised machine life is a change in estimate, applied prospectively."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Errors are restated in prior periods; changes in estimate are applied to current and future periods only.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-A-0022","t":"A company changes the estimated useful life of its equipment, and the change has a material effect on current-period income. Which disclosure is required in the period of change?","mc":{"s":"A company changes the estimated useful life of its equipment, and the change has a material effect on current-period income. Which disclosure is required in the period of change?","d":"","o":["The cumulative effect of the change on retained earnings at the beginning of the earliest period presented","A pro forma presentation of all prior years as if the new life had been used from the start","The auditor's letter of preferability for the new estimate","The effect of the change on income from continuing operations, net income and related per-share amounts"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 250-10-50-4","rat":"A change in estimate that affects several future periods, or is material, requires disclosure of its effect on income and per-share amounts.","ex":["That disclosure belongs to retrospective changes in principle, not to changes in estimate.","Estimates are not applied retrospectively, and no pro forma recast is required.","A preferability letter relates to changes in principle. An estimate change needs no preferability justification.","A change in estimate that affects several future periods, or is material, requires disclosure of its effect on income and per-share amounts."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A material change in estimate requires disclosing its effect on income from continuing operations, net income and per-share amounts.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"2":[{"i":"FAR-III-B-0001","t":"At what point must a company record a liability for a pending lawsuit brought against it?","mc":{"s":"At what point must a company record a liability for a pending lawsuit brought against it?","d":"","o":["When an unfavorable outcome is reasonably possible and the amount of the loss is at least partly known","When the plaintiff files the suit, whatever counsel's view of the likely outcome and amount","When the case is finally settled or a court judgment becomes final and payable","When an unfavorable outcome is probable and the amount of the loss can be reasonably estimated"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 450-20-25-2","rat":"Both conditions must be met at the date of the financial statements before a loss contingency is accrued.","ex":["A reasonably possible loss is disclosed but not accrued. The probability threshold is higher.","Filing alone does not trigger accrual. The company must assess probability and estimability of the loss.","Waiting for final resolution is too late. The accrual is made when the criteria are met, even while the case is pending.","Both conditions must be met at the date of the financial statements before a loss contingency is accrued."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Accrue a loss contingency when a loss is probable and reasonably estimable; otherwise disclose or ignore.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0002","t":"Marlowe Chemicals Inc. is defendant in a suit. Counsel believes a loss is probable and estimates it will fall between $400,000 and $900,000, with no amount in the range better than any other. What amount should Marlowe accrue at year-end?","mc":{"s":"Marlowe Chemicals Inc. is defendant in a suit. Counsel believes a loss is probable and estimates it will fall between $400,000 and $900,000, with no amount in the range better than any other. What amount should Marlowe accrue at year-end?","d":"","o":["$400,000","$500,000","$650,000","$900,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-30-1; ASC 450-20-25-2","rat":"When no amount within the range is a better estimate, the minimum is accrued and the additional reasonably possible exposure is disclosed.","ex":["When no amount within the range is a better estimate, the minimum is accrued and the additional reasonably possible exposure is disclosed.","Uses the width of the range, which has no basis in the measurement guidance.","Uses the midpoint. US GAAP does not use the midpoint when no amount in the range is a better estimate.","Uses the maximum. The minimum, not the maximum, is accrued when no best estimate exists."],"wt":["","calc","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"With no best estimate in a range, accrue the minimum and disclose the reasonably possible additional loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Loss is probable and estimable within a range","No amount in the range is a better estimate, so accrue the low end: 400,000","Disclose the reasonably possible additional loss up to 900,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0003","t":"A company faces a lawsuit, and counsel concludes that an unfavorable outcome is reasonably possible, and the loss could be as much as $2 million. How should the company report the matter?","mc":{"s":"A company faces a lawsuit, and counsel concludes that an unfavorable outcome is reasonably possible, and the loss could be as much as $2 million. How should the company report the matter?","d":"","o":["It discloses the nature of the contingency and an estimate of the possible loss or range, without accruing a liability","It accrues the full $2 million because the maximum exposure is known and disclosure is not enough","It accrues half of the amount because the outcome is more likely than a remote loss but less than probable, so the accrual is reduced","It makes no disclosure because the company has not been found liable in any court"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-50-3; ASC 450-20-50-4","rat":"A reasonably possible loss requires disclosure only, including an estimate or a statement that no estimate can be made.","ex":["A reasonably possible loss requires disclosure only, including an estimate or a statement that no estimate can be made.","Accrual requires that a loss be probable. A reasonably possible loss is not accrued.","Partial accruals based on rough probabilities are not permitted. The loss is either accrued in full as estimated or disclosed.","Disclosure is required for reasonably possible losses even before a court ruling."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A reasonably possible loss is disclosed, not accrued; a remote loss generally needs no disclosure.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0004","t":"At year-end, Sable Logistics Inc. faces three unrelated lawsuits. Suit 1: loss probable, estimated at $200,000. Suit 2: loss reasonably possible, estimated at $500,000. Suit 3: loss remote, estimated at $1,000,000. What total liability should Sable accrue?","mc":{"s":"At year-end, Sable Logistics Inc. faces three unrelated lawsuits. Suit 1: loss probable, estimated at $200,000. Suit 2: loss reasonably possible, estimated at $500,000. Suit 3: loss remote, estimated at $1,000,000. What total liability should Sable accrue?","d":"","o":["$200,000","$500,000","$700,000","$1,700,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-25-2; ASC 450-20-50-3","rat":"Only the probable and estimable loss is accrued.","ex":["Only the probable and estimable loss is accrued. Suit 2 is disclosed and suit 3 generally needs no disclosure.","Accrues the reasonably possible suit instead of the probable one, reversing the classification.","Adds the reasonably possible loss. Only probable losses are accrued.","Accrues all three. Remote and reasonably possible losses are not accrued."],"wt":["","trap","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Each contingency is evaluated separately; only probable and estimable losses are accrued.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Suit 1 probable and estimable: accrue 200,000","Suit 2 reasonably possible: disclose only","Suit 3 remote: no accrual"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0005","t":"Amber Appliances Inc. sells products with a standard one-year warranty that it cannot purchase separately. Warranty costs are estimated at 4% of sales. The warranty liability was $30,000 on January 1, 2026. Sales for 2026 were $2,000,000, and warranty claims paid in 2026 were $55,000. What is the warranty liability at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"Amber Appliances Inc. sells products with a standard one-year warranty that it cannot purchase separately. Warranty costs are estimated at 4% of sales. The warranty liability was $30,000 on January 1, 2026. Sales for 2026 were $2,000,000, and warranty claims paid in 2026 were $55,000. What is the warranty liability at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$25,000","$55,000","$80,000","$165,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 460-10-25-5; ASC 450-20-25-2","rat":"$30,000 + $80,000 accrued - $55,000 paid = $55,000.","ex":["Omits the opening liability of $30,000.","$30,000 + $80,000 accrued - $55,000 paid = $55,000.","Shows only the 2026 accrual and ignores both the opening balance and the payments.","Adds the claims paid. Payments settle the liability and reduce it."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Warranty liability equals opening balance plus the estimated expense accrued less claims paid.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["2026 accrual 2,000,000 x 4% = 80,000","Opening 30,000 + accrual 80,000 = 110,000","Less claims paid 55,000 = 55,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0006","t":"In 2026, Fenwick Media Inc. won a $700,000 court award against a competitor. The competitor has appealed, and the appeal will not be decided until 2027. How should Fenwick treat the award in its 2026 financial statements?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Fenwick Media Inc. won a $700,000 court award against a competitor. The competitor has appealed, and the appeal will not be decided until 2027. How should Fenwick treat the award in its 2026 financial statements?","d":"","o":["It recognizes a gain of $700,000 because a court has already ruled in its favor","It recognizes a gain equal to the probability-weighted amount expected after the appeal","It recognizes a deferred credit for $700,000 to be released to income if the appeal fails","It does not recognize a gain and may disclose the matter with care to avoid misleading implications about realization"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 450-30-25-1; ASC 450-30-50-1","rat":"Gain contingencies are not recognized until realized, though disclosure is permitted with caution.","ex":["A favorable judgment under appeal is still uncertain and is not realized. The gain is not recognized.","Expected-value recognition is not allowed for gains. Recognition waits until realization.","Recording the claim as a balance sheet item would reflect an asset before realization, contrary to the conservative treatment of gain contingencies.","Gain contingencies are not recognized until realized, though disclosure is permitted with caution."],"wt":["trap","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Gain contingencies are not recognized until realized or realizable; disclosure must avoid misleading implications.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0007","t":"Orion Warehousing Inc. recognizes a $300,000 loss from a fire during 2026. Its insurer has acknowledged coverage, and recovery of $200,000 is probable. What is the net pretax loss reported in 2026?","mc":{"s":"Orion Warehousing Inc. recognizes a $300,000 loss from a fire during 2026. Its insurer has acknowledged coverage, and recovery of $200,000 is probable. What is the net pretax loss reported in 2026?","d":"","o":["$300,000, because an insurance recovery is recognized only when cash is received from the insurer","$500,000, because the recovery is a separate additional expense of the period","$0, because the insurance recovery is assumed to offset the entire loss","$100,000, because the probable recovery is recognized as a receivable up to the loss recognized"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 450-30-25-1; ASC 410-30-35-8","rat":"A probable recovery of a recognized loss is recorded as an asset, so the net effect on income is $100,000.","ex":["A probable recovery of a recognized loss is recognized when realization is probable. It does not wait for cash.","A recovery is an asset that reduces the net loss. It is not an added cost.","The recovery of $200,000 covers only part of the $300,000 loss, and it cannot exceed the loss recognized.","A probable recovery of a recognized loss is recorded as an asset, so the net effect on income is $100,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A probable insurance recovery, up to the loss recognized, is recorded as a receivable and reduces the net loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Loss recognized 300,000","Probable recovery recognized 200,000, capped at the loss","Net pretax loss 100,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0008","t":"For a fee, Halcyon Corp. guarantees a $1,000,000 bank loan of an unrelated supplier. The supplier is financially sound, and payment under the guarantee is not probable. How does Halcyon account for the guarantee when issued?","mc":{"s":"For a fee, Halcyon Corp. guarantees a $1,000,000 bank loan of an unrelated supplier. The supplier is financially sound, and payment under the guarantee is not probable. How does Halcyon account for the guarantee when issued?","d":"","o":["It recognizes nothing because payment is not probable, and only records the fee as revenue when received","It recognizes a liability equal to the $1,000,000 maximum potential payment","It recognizes a liability at the fair value of the obligation it undertakes and discloses the guarantee","It only discloses the guarantee in the notes until the supplier defaults on the loan"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 460-10-25-4; ASC 460-10-30-2","rat":"A guarantor recognizes a liability at inception for the fair value of its stand-ready obligation, even if payment is not probable, and makes the required disclosures.","ex":["The probable-loss test governs contingent losses on existing conditions. The guarantee creates a separate stand-ready obligation measured at fair value at issuance.","The initial liability is the fair value of the guarantee, not the maximum potential payment. The maximum amount is disclosed.","A guarantor recognizes a liability at inception for the fair value of its stand-ready obligation, even if payment is not probable, and makes the required disclosures.","Disclosure alone is insufficient. A liability is recognized at the inception of the guarantee."],"wt":["trap","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A guarantor recognizes at inception a liability for the guarantee's fair value and discloses the maximum potential payment.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0009","t":"On November 1, 2026, Vireo Foods Inc. signed a noncancelable contract to buy raw materials for $500,000 for delivery in March 2027. At December 31, 2026, the market price for the same materials had fallen to $430,000, and the price is not expected to recover. What loss should Vireo recognize at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"On November 1, 2026, Vireo Foods Inc. signed a noncancelable contract to buy raw materials for $500,000 for delivery in March 2027. At December 31, 2026, the market price for the same materials had fallen to $430,000, and the price is not expected to recover. What loss should Vireo recognize at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$35,000","$70,000","$430,000","$500,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 330-10-35-17; ASC 440-10-50-2","rat":"A loss on a firm, noncancelable purchase commitment is recognized when the contract price exceeds market, as inventory losses are: $500,000 - $430,000 = $70,000.","ex":["Recognizes half of the decline. The full decline is recognized when a loss on a firm commitment is expected.","A loss on a firm, noncancelable purchase commitment is recognized when the contract price exceeds market, as inventory losses are: $500,000 - $430,000 = $70,000.","Uses the market value as the loss. The loss is the difference between contract and market prices.","Recognizes the full contract price. Only the excess of contract price over current market value is a loss."],"wt":["trap","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A firm purchase commitment loss is the excess of contract price over market, recognized when the decline is not expected to recover.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Contract price 500,000","Market price at year-end 430,000","Loss 500,000 - 430,000 = 70,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0010","t":"In December 2026, Trellis Motors Inc. announced a recall. It is probable that 12,000 vehicles will be returned, and the best estimate of cost per vehicle is $45. What liability should be recorded at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"In December 2026, Trellis Motors Inc. announced a recall. It is probable that 12,000 vehicles will be returned, and the best estimate of cost per vehicle is $45. What liability should be recorded at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$540,000","$270,000","$45","$0, because the cost will be incurred in 2027 when repairs are made"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-25-2; ASC 450-20-30-1","rat":"The loss is probable and estimable, so the best estimate of $540,000 is accrued.","ex":["The loss is probable and estimable, so the best estimate of $540,000 is accrued.","Accrues half the estimated cost. Once the loss is probable, the best estimate is accrued in full.","Records the cost of a single vehicle rather than the estimated total number returned.","Cost timing does not delay recognition. The obligation exists at year-end and is estimable."],"wt":["","trap","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Accrue the best estimate of a probable, estimable recall cost in the period the obligation arises.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Estimated units returned 12,000","Cost per unit 45","Liability 12,000 x 45 = 540,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0011","t":"Pinnacle Industries Inc. must clean up a contaminated site. Remediation is probable, costs are estimated at $2,400,000, and payments will be made over the next five years, but neither the amount nor the timing of each payment is fixed or reliably determinable. How should the liability be measured?","mc":{"s":"Pinnacle Industries Inc. must clean up a contaminated site. Remediation is probable, costs are estimated at $2,400,000, and payments will be made over the next five years, but neither the amount nor the timing of each payment is fixed or reliably determinable. How should the liability be measured?","d":"","o":["At the present value of $2,400,000 using the company's incremental borrowing rate","At the present value using a risk-free rate, because remediation is government-mandated","At the undiscounted best estimate of $2,400,000, because neither amount nor timing is fixed or reliably determinable","At the minimum of the range, since the final amount cannot be predicted with certainty"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 410-30-35-12; ASC 450-20-30-1","rat":"Discounting of environmental remediation liabilities is allowed only when the aggregate amount and timing of cash payments are fixed or reliably determinable.","ex":["Discounting is not permitted because the amount and timing are uncertain. An incremental borrowing rate is not the deciding factor.","A government mandate does not permit discounting. The conditions of fixed or reliably determinable amount and timing must be met.","Discounting of environmental remediation liabilities is allowed only when the aggregate amount and timing of cash payments are fixed or reliably determinable.","The best estimate is used when there is one. The minimum is used only when no amount in a range is a better estimate."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Environmental liabilities are discounted only if the amount and timing of payments are fixed or reliably determinable.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0012","t":"At December 31, 2025, Kestrel Software Inc. accrued $300,000 for a probable lawsuit loss, and the 2025 statements were issued in March 2026. The suit was settled in September 2026 for $340,000. No new information about conditions at the end of 2025 was discovered other than the settlement itself. What expense is recognized in 2026 for the difference?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2025, Kestrel Software Inc. accrued $300,000 for a probable lawsuit loss, and the 2025 statements were issued in March 2026. The suit was settled in September 2026 for $340,000. No new information about conditions at the end of 2025 was discovered other than the settlement itself. What expense is recognized in 2026 for the difference?","d":"","o":["$40,000","$80,000","$300,000","$340,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-35-1; ASC 250-10-45-17","rat":"The difference between the settlement and the accrual is a change in estimate, recognized in the period of settlement: $340,000 - $300,000 = $40,000.","ex":["The difference between the settlement and the accrual is a change in estimate, recognized in the period of settlement: $340,000 - $300,000 = $40,000.","Doubles the difference with no basis. The excess of settlement over accrual is expensed once.","Recognizes the previously accrued amount again. The accrual is settled against the liability, not expensed a second time.","Recognizes the full settlement as 2026 expense, forgetting that $300,000 was already accrued in 2025."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The difference between a loss settlement and the earlier accrual is a change in estimate recognized when settled.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Settlement 340,000","Previously accrued liability 300,000","Additional expense in 2026 = 40,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0013","t":"Zephyr Mining Inc. accrues a probable litigation loss of $800,000 in 2026. The loss is deductible for tax only when paid, expected in 2027. The enacted tax rate is 25%, and realization of the resulting tax benefit is more likely than not. What is the effect of the accrual on 2026 net income?","mc":{"s":"Zephyr Mining Inc. accrues a probable litigation loss of $800,000 in 2026. The loss is deductible for tax only when paid, expected in 2027. The enacted tax rate is 25%, and realization of the resulting tax benefit is more likely than not. What is the effect of the accrual on 2026 net income?","d":"","o":["$200,000","$600,000","$800,000","$1,000,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 450-20-25-2; ASC 740-10-25-20","rat":"The accrual creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset of $200,000, so the after-tax reduction in income is $600,000.","ex":["Reports only the tax effect, not the after-tax loss.","The accrual creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset of $200,000, so the after-tax reduction in income is $600,000.","Ignores the deferred tax benefit. A deductible temporary difference produces a deferred tax asset that offsets part of the loss.","Adds the tax rate instead of deducting the tax benefit."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A loss accrued for books but deductible when paid creates a deferred tax asset, reducing the after-tax loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Pretax loss accrued 800,000","Deferred tax asset 800,000 x 25% = 200,000 (tax benefit)","After-tax reduction 800,000 - 200,000 = 600,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0014","t":"A company learns that a customer may have a claim arising from a product defect, but no claim has yet been made. When must the company disclose the possible claim in its financial statements?","mc":{"s":"A company learns that a customer may have a claim arising from a product defect, but no claim has yet been made. When must the company disclose the possible claim in its financial statements?","d":"","o":["When it is probable that the claim will be asserted and an unfavorable outcome is reasonably possible","Only after the claimant has filed suit or made a written demand for payment","Whenever it is reasonably possible that the customer will assert any claim, even if the outcome would be favorable","Only if the company has already established a reserve for the estimated loss"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-50-6","rat":"Disclosure of an unasserted claim is required if assertion is probable and there is at least a reasonable possibility of an unfavorable outcome.","ex":["Disclosure of an unasserted claim is required if assertion is probable and there is at least a reasonable possibility of an unfavorable outcome.","Unasserted claims may require disclosure before any assertion. Waiting for a demand would omit relevant information.","The test combines a probable assertion and a reasonably possible unfavorable outcome. A merely possible assertion is not enough.","The presence of an accrual is not required for disclosure. Disclosure is based on probability of assertion and outcome."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Disclose an unasserted claim if assertion is probable and an unfavorable outcome is at least reasonably possible.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0015","t":"A loss on pending litigation is probable, but management cannot reasonably estimate the amount. Which is required?","mc":{"s":"A loss on pending litigation is probable, but management cannot reasonably estimate the amount. Which is required?","d":"","o":["Accrue the largest amount that the plaintiff has claimed and disclose the basis","Accrue an amount equal to average settlements in similar cases without any disclosure","Report nothing until the amount becomes reasonably estimable, since no loss has been measured","Disclose the nature of the contingency and state that an estimate of the loss cannot be made, without accruing a liability"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 450-20-50-3; ASC 450-20-50-4","rat":"If the amount cannot be reasonably estimated, no accrual is made, and the disclosure includes a statement that the estimate cannot be made.","ex":["An accrual requires a reasonable estimate. The claimed amount is not the company's estimate of loss.","Without a reasonable estimate for this case, the company cannot accrue, and disclosure is required for a probable loss.","Disclosure is required for a probable loss even when the amount cannot be estimated.","If the amount cannot be reasonably estimated, no accrual is made, and the disclosure includes a statement that the estimate cannot be made."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A probable but inestimable loss is disclosed, with a statement that an estimate cannot be made.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0016","t":"Cobalt Shipping Inc. is sued after an accident. A loss is probable, in a range of $800,000 to $1,400,000, with a best estimate of $1,000,000. The insurer has acknowledged coverage, and it is probable that $600,000 will be recovered. What is the net pretax loss recognized, and what liability is presented?","mc":{"s":"Cobalt Shipping Inc. is sued after an accident. A loss is probable, in a range of $800,000 to $1,400,000, with a best estimate of $1,000,000. The insurer has acknowledged coverage, and it is probable that $600,000 will be recovered. What is the net pretax loss recognized, and what liability is presented?","d":"","o":["$400,000 net loss, with a $1,000,000 liability and a $600,000 receivable presented gross","$200,000 net loss, using the minimum of the range less the recovery","$1,000,000 net loss, because insurance recoveries may not be recognized until received","$400,000 net loss, with a net liability of $400,000 presented after offsetting the receivable"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 450-20-30-1; ASC 410-30-35-8; ASC 210-20-45-1","rat":"The best estimate of $1,000,000 is accrued, and the probable recovery of $600,000 is recognized as a separate asset, giving a net loss of $400,000.","ex":["The best estimate of $1,000,000 is accrued, and the probable recovery of $600,000 is recognized as a separate asset, giving a net loss of $400,000.","The minimum applies only when no amount in the range is a better estimate. Here a best estimate exists.","A probable recovery of a recognized loss is recognized as a receivable, reducing the net loss.","The right of offset is not met for insurance claims, so the liability and the receivable are presented gross."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Accrue the best estimate in the range; record a probable insurance recovery as a separate asset, not netted against the liability.","from":"2026-01-01","steps":["Best estimate exists, so accrue 1,000,000 (not the minimum 800,000)","Probable recovery 600,000 recognized as an asset","Net pretax loss 1,000,000 - 600,000 = 400,000; presented gross"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0017","t":"At year-end, four claims exist against Rhodes Supply Co. Which one requires an accrual in the year-end financial statements?","mc":{"s":"At year-end, four claims exist against Rhodes Supply Co. Which one requires an accrual in the year-end financial statements?","d":"","o":["A patent claim in which loss is reasonably possible and counsel estimates the loss at $250,000","A contract dispute in which loss is remote and the claimed amount is $1,000,000","A tax claim in which loss is probable but counsel cannot estimate any amount in a range","A customer-injury suit in which loss is probable and counsel estimates the loss at $90,000"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 450-20-25-2","rat":"The loss is probable and the amount is reasonably estimable, so it is accrued.","ex":["A reasonably possible loss is disclosed but not accrued.","A remote loss is not accrued and generally not disclosed.","A probable loss without a reasonable estimate is disclosed, not accrued.","The loss is probable and the amount is reasonably estimable, so it is accrued."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A loss requires accrual only when it is both probable and reasonably estimable.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-B-0018","t":"Bristol Energy Inc. entered into a take-or-pay contract obliging it to pay for a minimum quantity of natural gas each year for ten years, whether or not it takes delivery. The contract is not recorded as a liability. What is required in Bristol's financial statements?","mc":{"s":"Bristol Energy Inc. entered into a take-or-pay contract obliging it to pay for a minimum quantity of natural gas each year for ten years, whether or not it takes delivery. The contract is not recorded as a liability. What is required in Bristol's financial statements?","d":"","o":["No disclosure, since a purchase not yet taken is an executory contract that does not affect the financial statements until goods are received","Recognition of a liability for the total of the ten years of minimum payments at signing","Disclosure of the nature and term of the obligation and the fixed and determinable amounts for each of the next five years","Disclosure only of the amount paid during the current year, without future commitments"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 440-10-50-4","rat":"An unrecorded unconditional purchase obligation must be disclosed with its nature, term and the minimum annual amounts for the next five years.","ex":["Unrecorded unconditional purchase obligations that meet the criteria require disclosure even though they are executory.","The obligation is not recorded as a liability at signing for an executory contract. It is disclosed unless a loss arises.","An unrecorded unconditional purchase obligation must be disclosed with its nature, term and the minimum annual amounts for the next five years.","Future commitments for the next five years are the key disclosure, in addition to the amounts paid."],"wt":["trap","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unrecorded unconditional purchase obligations are disclosed, including fixed and determinable payments for each of the next five years.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"3":[{"i":"FAR-III-C-0001","t":"Which sequence lists the steps of the revenue recognition model in the correct order?","mc":{"s":"Which sequence lists the steps of the revenue recognition model in the correct order?","d":"","o":["Identify the contract; determine the transaction price; identify performance obligations; recognize revenue; allocate the price","Identify performance obligations; identify the contract; allocate the price; determine the transaction price; recognize revenue","Determine the transaction price; identify the contract; recognize revenue; identify performance obligations; allocate the price","Identify the contract; identify performance obligations; determine the transaction price; allocate the price; recognize revenue as obligations are satisfied"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 606-10-05-4","rat":"The model moves from the contract to the obligations, then price, allocation and finally recognition.","ex":["Performance obligations are identified before the price is allocated, and allocation comes before recognition.","The contract must be identified first, and the transaction price must be determined before it can be allocated.","This order places recognition before the obligations are identified and the price allocated.","The model moves from the contract to the obligations, then price, allocation and finally recognition."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Revenue model: contract, performance obligations, transaction price, allocation, recognition when obligations are satisfied.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0002","t":"An entity satisfies a performance obligation over time, rather than at a point in time, if at least one of three criteria is met. Which one is a valid criterion?","mc":{"s":"An entity satisfies a performance obligation over time, rather than at a point in time, if at least one of three criteria is met. Which one is a valid criterion?","d":"","o":["The entity has a right to payment for the goods only after the customer has accepted the delivery","The entity has a history of being paid in installments over the life of a multi-year contract","The entity's performance creates or enhances an asset that the customer controls as the asset is created or enhanced","The contract price is fixed at inception and cannot be adjusted for changes in costs"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-27","rat":"This is one of the three over-time criteria, along with simultaneous receipt and consumption of benefits, and an asset with no alternative use plus an enforceable right to payment.","ex":["A right to payment matters only if it is enforceable for performance completed to date and the asset has no alternative use.","A payment pattern does not by itself indicate over-time transfer of control.","This is one of the three over-time criteria, along with simultaneous receipt and consumption of benefits, and an asset with no alternative use plus an enforceable right to payment.","Fixed pricing does not affect the timing of control transfer."],"wt":["trap","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Revenue is recognized over time if the customer simultaneously benefits, controls the asset as created, or the asset has no alternative use with payment right.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0003","t":"Nordic Systems Inc. sells a software license, installation and one year of support to a customer for a total of $900,000. Standalone selling prices are $600,000 for the license, $200,000 for installation and $200,000 for support. Each is a distinct performance obligation. What amount is allocated to the license?","mc":{"s":"Nordic Systems Inc. sells a software license, installation and one year of support to a customer for a total of $900,000. Standalone selling prices are $600,000 for the license, $200,000 for installation and $200,000 for support. Each is a distinct performance obligation. What amount is allocated to the license?","d":"","o":["$300,000","$500,000","$540,000","$600,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-31; ASC 606-10-32-28","rat":"$900,000 x $600,000 / $1,000,000 = $540,000.","ex":["Divides the price equally among the three obligations rather than by relative SSP.","Assigns the entire discount to the license by treating installation and support at full SSP.","$900,000 x $600,000 / $1,000,000 = $540,000. The $100,000 discount is spread proportionally.","Uses the standalone selling price and ignores the discount. The transaction price is allocated in proportion to relative SSPs."],"wt":["calc","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Allocate the transaction price to each obligation in proportion to standalone selling prices.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total SSP 600,000 + 200,000 + 200,000 = 1,000,000","License share 600,000 / 1,000,000 = 60%","Allocated 900,000 x 60% = 540,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0004","t":"Arbor Builders Inc. signs a contract to build a facility for $900,000, plus a $100,000 bonus if it finishes by September 30. Management estimates a 60% probability of earning the bonus, but weather delays have hit similar projects in most past years, and the outcome depends on factors outside its control. What transaction price should Arbor use at contract inception?","mc":{"s":"Arbor Builders Inc. signs a contract to build a facility for $900,000, plus a $100,000 bonus if it finishes by September 30. Management estimates a 60% probability of earning the bonus, but weather delays have hit similar projects in most past years, and the outcome depends on factors outside its control. What transaction price should Arbor use at contract inception?","d":"","o":["$900,000","$940,000","$960,000","$1,000,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-5; ASC 606-10-32-11","rat":"The bonus is a binary outcome, and given weather risk, it is not probable that a significant revenue reversal will not occur.","ex":["The bonus is a binary outcome, and given weather risk, it is not probable that a significant revenue reversal will not occur. The bonus is constrained and excluded until uncertainty resolves.","Adds the probability of missing the bonus, reversing the logic of the estimate.","Uses the expected value. Even when expected value is the estimation method, the constraint can limit the amount included.","Includes the whole bonus. Variable consideration is included only to the extent it is probable that a significant reversal of cumulative revenue will not occur; with weather risk outside the entity's control and poor past experience, that condition is not met."],"wt":["","calc","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Variable consideration is included only to the extent a significant revenue reversal is not probable.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Estimate of variable consideration (most likely amount for a binary outcome or expected value): 100,000 or 60,000","Constraint: reversal risk is high because the outcome depends on weather","Include none of the bonus; transaction price 900,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0005","t":"Kelso Sporting Goods Inc. sells products to a distributor for $500,000, with a right of return within 60 days. The goods cost $300,000, and Kelso expects 8% of the goods to be returned, with reliable data to support the estimate. What revenue does Kelso recognize at the time of sale?","mc":{"s":"Kelso Sporting Goods Inc. sells products to a distributor for $500,000, with a right of return within 60 days. The goods cost $300,000, and Kelso expects 8% of the goods to be returned, with reliable data to support the estimate. What revenue does Kelso recognize at the time of sale?","d":"","o":["$40,000","$460,000","$500,000","$540,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-22; ASC 606-10-32-10","rat":"Revenue is recognized only for goods not expected to be returned: $500,000 x 92% = $460,000.","ex":["Recognizes only the returns portion.","Revenue is recognized only for goods not expected to be returned: $500,000 x 92% = $460,000. The remaining $40,000 is a refund liability.","Recognizes full revenue and ignores the expected returns. A refund liability is recorded for the portion expected to be returned.","Adds the expected returns to revenue, reversing the direction of the adjustment."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Recognize revenue only for goods not expected to be returned and record a refund liability for the rest.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Expected returns 500,000 x 8% = 40,000 (refund liability)","Revenue 500,000 - 40,000 = 460,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0006","t":"Using the facts of a sale of $500,000 at a cost of $300,000 with 8% of goods expected to be returned, Kelso Sporting Goods Inc. recognizes a refund liability and a return asset. What gross profit is recognized at the time of sale?","mc":{"s":"Using the facts of a sale of $500,000 at a cost of $300,000 with 8% of goods expected to be returned, Kelso Sporting Goods Inc. recognizes a refund liability and a return asset. What gross profit is recognized at the time of sale?","d":"","o":["$160,000","$184,000","$200,000","$224,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-23; ASC 606-10-32-10","rat":"Revenue $460,000 less cost of goods sold $276,000 (the $24,000 cost of goods expected back is a return asset) = $184,000.","ex":["Deducts the full cost of $300,000. The cost of goods expected to be returned is recorded as a return asset, not expensed.","Revenue $460,000 less cost of goods sold $276,000 (the $24,000 cost of goods expected back is a return asset) = $184,000.","Ignores the returns on both revenue and cost.","Uses full revenue but the reduced cost. Revenue must also exclude expected returns."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Revenue and cost of sales are both reduced for expected returns; the returned goods' cost is a separate asset.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Revenue 500,000 x 92% = 460,000","Cost of goods sold 300,000 x 92% = 276,000 (return asset 24,000)","Gross profit 460,000 - 276,000 = 184,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0007","t":"Lumen Marketplace Inc. operates a website where vendors sell goods directly to shoppers. Lumen does not take title, bears no inventory risk and does not set prices. It collects $2,500,000 of gross sales during 2026, remits the vendors' share, and keeps a 12% commission. What revenue does Lumen report for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Lumen Marketplace Inc. operates a website where vendors sell goods directly to shoppers. Lumen does not take title, bears no inventory risk and does not set prices. It collects $2,500,000 of gross sales during 2026, remits the vendors' share, and keeps a 12% commission. What revenue does Lumen report for 2026?","d":"","o":["$300,000","$2,200,000","$2,500,000","$2,800,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-36; ASC 606-10-55-37A","rat":"Lumen is an agent because it does not control the goods before transfer.","ex":["Lumen is an agent because it does not control the goods before transfer. Revenue is the net commission earned: $2,500,000 x 12% = $300,000.","Reports the amount remitted to vendors. That is the vendors' revenue, not Lumen's.","Reports gross billings. Gross reporting applies only to a principal that controls the goods before they transfer to the customer.","Adds the commission to gross sales, which double-counts the commission."],"wt":["","calc","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An entity that does not control the good before transfer is an agent and reports revenue net as its fee or commission.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Agent: no title, no inventory risk, no pricing discretion","Revenue is the commission 2,500,000 x 12% = 300,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0008","t":"Bayfront Engineering Inc. has a $3,000,000 contract to build a facility for a customer who controls the asset as it is built. Estimated total cost is $2,400,000. Costs incurred were $720,000 in year 1 and $840,000 in year 2, with no change in the estimate. Using the cost-to-cost method, what revenue is recognized in year 2?","mc":{"s":"Bayfront Engineering Inc. has a $3,000,000 contract to build a facility for a customer who controls the asset as it is built. Estimated total cost is $2,400,000. Costs incurred were $720,000 in year 1 and $840,000 in year 2, with no change in the estimate. Using the cost-to-cost method, what revenue is recognized in year 2?","d":"","o":["$840,000","$1,050,000","$1,500,000","$1,950,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-31; ASC 606-10-55-20","rat":"Cumulative progress is $1,560,000 / $2,400,000 = 65%, so cumulative revenue is $1,950,000.","ex":["Reports the cost incurred as revenue. This would be a zero-margin recognition.","Cumulative progress is $1,560,000 / $2,400,000 = 65%, so cumulative revenue is $1,950,000. Less year 1 revenue of $900,000 = $1,050,000.","Applies year 2 costs as a share of the remaining costs to the total contract price instead of the price for the remaining work.","Reports the cumulative revenue instead of the year 2 amount. Prior-year revenue must be deducted."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Progress = costs to date / total estimated costs; period revenue = cumulative revenue less prior recognized revenue.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Year 1 revenue 3,000,000 x 720,000 / 2,400,000 = 900,000","Cumulative revenue 3,000,000 x 1,560,000 / 2,400,000 = 1,950,000","Year 2 revenue 1,950,000 - 900,000 = 1,050,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0009","t":"Stonehaven Construction Inc. has a $3,000,000 contract for a customer-controlled asset and uses the cost-to-cost method. At the end of year 1, costs incurred were $720,000 against an estimated total of $2,400,000. During year 2, it incurred $900,000 more, and revised the estimated total cost to $2,700,000. What revenue is recognized in year 2?","mc":{"s":"Stonehaven Construction Inc. has a $3,000,000 contract for a customer-controlled asset and uses the cost-to-cost method. At the end of year 1, costs incurred were $720,000 against an estimated total of $2,400,000. During year 2, it incurred $900,000 more, and revised the estimated total cost to $2,700,000. What revenue is recognized in year 2?","d":"","o":["$900,000","$1,000,000","$1,125,000","$1,800,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-35; ASC 250-10-45-17","rat":"Cumulative progress is $1,620,000 / $2,700,000 = 60%, so cumulative revenue is $1,800,000.","ex":["Cumulative progress is $1,620,000 / $2,700,000 = 60%, so cumulative revenue is $1,800,000. Year 1 revenue was $900,000, so year 2 revenue is $900,000. The change in estimate is absorbed through a cumulative catch-up.","Applies only the year 2 costs to the revised total. The cumulative percentage must be applied and prior revenue deducted.","Uses the original estimate of total cost. The revised estimate is applied prospectively through a cumulative catch-up.","Reports cumulative revenue as if it were year 2 revenue and does not deduct the $900,000 already recognized."],"wt":["","calc","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in estimated total cost changes the progress percentage; revenue is adjusted through a cumulative catch-up in the current period.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Year 1 revenue 3,000,000 x 720,000 / 2,400,000 = 900,000","Cumulative cost 720,000 + 900,000 = 1,620,000; progress 1,620,000 / 2,700,000 = 60%; cumulative revenue 1,800,000","Year 2 revenue 1,800,000 - 900,000 = 900,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0010","t":"Redstone Fabrication Inc. has a $3,000,000 fixed-price contract for a customer-controlled asset and uses cost-to-cost. At the end of year 1, costs incurred were $990,000, and the estimated total cost is $3,300,000, which exceeds the contract price. What gross loss does Redstone report for year 1?","mc":{"s":"Redstone Fabrication Inc. has a $3,000,000 fixed-price contract for a customer-controlled asset and uses cost-to-cost. At the end of year 1, costs incurred were $990,000, and the estimated total cost is $3,300,000, which exceeds the contract price. What gross loss does Redstone report for year 1?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$210,000","$300,000","$900,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 605-35-25-45","rat":"Year 1 revenue of $900,000 less cost of $990,000 is a $90,000 loss, and a further $210,000 provision is recorded for the remaining expected loss, so the entire $300,000 loss is recognized immediately.","ex":["Recognizes only the year 1 revenue and cost difference. The whole expected contract loss is recognized when it becomes evident.","Recognizes only the additional provision for the remaining loss, omitting the loss from year 1 revenue and cost.","Year 1 revenue of $900,000 less cost of $990,000 is a $90,000 loss, and a further $210,000 provision is recorded for the remaining expected loss, so the entire $300,000 loss is recognized immediately.","Confuses the revenue recognized with the loss."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When total expected costs exceed the contract price, the entire anticipated loss is recognized in the period it becomes evident.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Year 1 revenue 3,000,000 x 990,000 / 3,300,000 = 900,000","Total expected loss 3,300,000 - 3,000,000 = 300,000","Recognize the whole 300,000 in year 1 (90,000 from operations + 210,000 provision)"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0011","t":"Copperline Cafe Inc. sold $200,000 of gift cards in 2026 and expects 10% of the value to go unredeemed, with reliable data on customer redemption patterns. Customers redeemed $120,000 of the cards in 2026. How much revenue does Copperline recognize in 2026 from these cards?","mc":{"s":"Copperline Cafe Inc. sold $200,000 of gift cards in 2026 and expects 10% of the value to go unredeemed, with reliable data on customer redemption patterns. Customers redeemed $120,000 of the cards in 2026. How much revenue does Copperline recognize in 2026 from these cards?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$120,000","$133,333","$200,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-48; ASC 606-10-55-46","rat":"Expected redemptions are $180,000.","ex":["Recognizes only the breakage amount, ignoring the revenue from redemptions.","Recognizes only the amount redeemed and delays all breakage until the cards expire.","Expected redemptions are $180,000. Breakage is recognized in proportion to redemption: $120,000 / $180,000 x $200,000 = $133,333.","Recognizes the full sale on issuance. A gift card sale is deferred until redemption or breakage."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Expected breakage is recognized proportionately as the customer redeems, when it can be reliably estimated.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Expected redemption 200,000 x 90% = 180,000","Percentage redeemed 120,000 / 180,000 = 66.67%","Revenue 200,000 x 66.67% = 133,333"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0012","t":"On January 1, 2026, Orbit Software Inc. grants a customer a three-year license to use its software as it exists, for a fixed fee of $800,000 paid up front. The software has significant standalone functionality, and Orbit has no further obligations. How much revenue is recognized in 2026?","mc":{"s":"On January 1, 2026, Orbit Software Inc. grants a customer a three-year license to use its software as it exists, for a fixed fee of $800,000 paid up front. The software has significant standalone functionality, and Orbit has no further obligations. How much revenue is recognized in 2026?","d":"","o":["$266,667","$400,000","$533,333","$800,000"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-58; ASC 606-10-55-58C","rat":"A license to functional IP is a right to use recognized at the point in time control transfers, when the customer can use and benefit from it.","ex":["Recognizes the license ratably over three years, which applies to a right to access symbolic IP, not to functional IP.","Recognizes half of the fee as revenue and defers the rest, without a basis for the deferral.","Recognizes two-thirds of the fee, with no basis in the licensing guidance.","A license to functional IP is a right to use recognized at the point in time control transfers, when the customer can use and benefit from it. All $800,000 is recognized in 2026."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A license to functional IP is recognized at a point in time when the customer can use and benefit from the license.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Functional IP (right to use): revenue at the point in time control transfers","License period begins January 1, 2026: recognize the whole 800,000 in 2026"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0013","t":"Brightside Brands Inc. licenses its trade name and logo to a franchisee for five years for $600,000 paid up front. The franchisee's business benefits from Brightside's ongoing advertising, which affects the value of the brand, and the license has no standalone functionality. How much revenue does Brightside recognize in the first year?","mc":{"s":"Brightside Brands Inc. licenses its trade name and logo to a franchisee for five years for $600,000 paid up front. The franchisee's business benefits from Brightside's ongoing advertising, which affects the value of the brand, and the license has no standalone functionality. How much revenue does Brightside recognize in the first year?","d":"","o":["$100,000","$120,000","$300,000","$600,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-58A; ASC 606-10-55-59","rat":"Symbolic IP is a right to access that is affected by the licensor's ongoing activities, so revenue is recognized over time: $600,000 / 5 = $120,000 per year.","ex":["Divides the fee over six years, adding a year to the license term.","Symbolic IP is a right to access that is affected by the licensor's ongoing activities, so revenue is recognized over time: $600,000 / 5 = $120,000 per year.","Recognizes half of the fee in year one with no basis in the time pattern of the license.","Recognizes the entire fee at the start. A right to access IP is satisfied over the license period."],"wt":["calc","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Symbolic IP whose value depends on the licensor's continuing activities is a right to access recognized over the license term.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Symbolic IP with ongoing licensor activity: performance obligation satisfied over time","Straight-line over 5 years: 600,000 / 5 = 120,000 per year"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0014","t":"Vantage Media Inc. licenses a film library to a broadcaster for three years. The license fee is a royalty of 5% of the broadcaster's advertising sales from the library, with no fixed fee. The broadcaster's 2026 advertising sales from the library were $4,000,000. How much revenue does Vantage recognize for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Vantage Media Inc. licenses a film library to a broadcaster for three years. The license fee is a royalty of 5% of the broadcaster's advertising sales from the library, with no fixed fee. The broadcaster's 2026 advertising sales from the library were $4,000,000. How much revenue does Vantage recognize for 2026?","d":"","o":["$66,667","$200,000","$600,000","$4,000,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-65","rat":"Sales-based royalties from a license of IP are recognized when the sales occur (later of the sale and satisfaction of the obligation): $4,000,000 x 5% = $200,000.","ex":["Spreads one year of royalties over three years, although the amount relates to sales in 2026.","Sales-based royalties from a license of IP are recognized when the sales occur (later of the sale and satisfaction of the obligation): $4,000,000 x 5% = $200,000.","Recognizes three years of expected royalties at the start. Uncertain future sales are not estimated for this royalty exception.","Recognizes the licensee's entire sales as revenue."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Sales-based royalties on licensed IP are recognized as the underlying sales occur.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sales-based royalty exception applies to a license of IP","Revenue 2026 = 5% x 4,000,000 = 200,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0015","t":"Kestrel Machinery Inc. delivers equipment to a customer on January 1, 2026, and control transfers on that date. The customer must pay $1,210,000 on December 31, 2027. An interest rate of 10% reflects a separate financing transaction between the parties. What is the revenue recognized on delivery?","mc":{"s":"Kestrel Machinery Inc. delivers equipment to a customer on January 1, 2026, and control transfers on that date. The customer must pay $1,210,000 on December 31, 2027. An interest rate of 10% reflects a separate financing transaction between the parties. What is the revenue recognized on delivery?","d":"","o":["$1,000,000","$1,089,000","$1,100,000","$1,210,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-15; ASC 606-10-32-19","rat":"The contract has a significant financing component, so revenue is the present value of the promised consideration: $1,210,000 / 1.10^2 = $1,000,000.","ex":["The contract has a significant financing component, so revenue is the present value of the promised consideration: $1,210,000 / 1.10^2 = $1,000,000. The difference is interest income over two years.","Applies a simple 10% reduction to the price instead of discounting at compound interest for two years.","Discounts for one year only although payment is due after two years.","Recognizes the nominal amount with no time value adjustment. A financing component this long must be separated."],"wt":["","calc","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When payment timing gives a significant financing benefit, revenue equals the present value of the consideration; the difference is interest.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Discount 1,210,000 for two years at 10%","1,210,000 / 1.21 = 1,000,000 revenue on delivery","Interest income accrues to December 31, 2027 (210,000 in total)"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0016","t":"Tidewater Supply Inc. agreed to sell 100 units at $10 each. After 60 units were delivered, the customer and Tidewater modified the contract to add 40 more units at $8 each, which is below the standalone selling price. The remaining goods are distinct from those already delivered. What total revenue is recognized for the 80 units still to be delivered?","mc":{"s":"Tidewater Supply Inc. agreed to sell 100 units at $10 each. After 60 units were delivered, the customer and Tidewater modified the contract to add 40 more units at $8 each, which is below the standalone selling price. The remaining goods are distinct from those already delivered. What total revenue is recognized for the 80 units still to be delivered?","d":"","o":["$320","$640","$720","$800"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-13; ASC 606-10-25-10","rat":"The added price is not the standalone selling price, so the modification is treated as termination of the old contract and creation of a new one.","ex":["Counts only the added units and omits the remaining units from the original contract.","Prices all 80 remaining units at the new $8 price. The unchanged 40 units still carry the original price.","The added price is not the standalone selling price, so the modification is treated as termination of the old contract and creation of a new one. Remaining consideration is $400 + $320 = $720.","Prices all 80 remaining units at the old $10 price, ignoring the new lower price for the added units."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If added distinct goods are not priced at standalone selling price, the modification is treated prospectively, combining remaining and added consideration.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Remaining original units 100 - 60 = 40 at $10 = 400","Added units 40 at $8 = 320","Total consideration for remaining 80 units 400 + 320 = 720"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0017","t":"A contract to deliver 100 machines at $500 each is modified, adding 20 more machines that are distinct. The price of the added machines is $470, which reflects the standalone selling price for those machines in the circumstances. How is the modification accounted for?","mc":{"s":"A contract to deliver 100 machines at $500 each is modified, adding 20 more machines that are distinct. The price of the added machines is $470, which reflects the standalone selling price for those machines in the circumstances. How is the modification accounted for?","d":"","o":["As a separate contract, because it adds distinct goods at their standalone selling price","As a termination of the original contract and creation of a new one, with the combined consideration allocated to remaining goods","As a cumulative catch-up adjustment to the original contract price and progress","As a change in estimate that reduces the price of the original 100 machines to a blended average"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-12","rat":"A modification that adds distinct goods at a price reflecting standalone selling price is a separate contract, and the original contract is unchanged.","ex":["A modification that adds distinct goods at a price reflecting standalone selling price is a separate contract, and the original contract is unchanged.","This treatment applies when added goods are distinct but not priced at standalone selling price.","The catch-up method applies to modifications of goods that are not distinct from those already delivered.","The added machines are separately priced, so prior contract terms do not change."],"wt":["","concept","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Adding distinct goods at standalone selling price creates a separate contract.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0018","t":"Fairhaven Furniture Inc. shipped $300,000 of sofas to a retailer under a consignment arrangement. Fairhaven keeps title until the retailer sells to a customer and can require return of the goods. By year-end, the retailer had sold to customers sofas for which Fairhaven's agreed price is $180,000. How much revenue does Fairhaven recognize?","mc":{"s":"Fairhaven Furniture Inc. shipped $300,000 of sofas to a retailer under a consignment arrangement. Fairhaven keeps title until the retailer sells to a customer and can require return of the goods. By year-end, the retailer had sold to customers sofas for which Fairhaven's agreed price is $180,000. How much revenue does Fairhaven recognize?","d":"","o":["$120,000","$180,000","$300,000","$480,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-79; ASC 606-10-55-80","rat":"On consignment, control has not transferred at delivery to the retailer.","ex":["Recognizes revenue on the goods still unsold, the opposite of the correct amount.","On consignment, control has not transferred at delivery to the retailer. Revenue is recognized when the retailer sells to the end customer: $180,000.","Recognizes revenue on shipment to the consignee. Control has not transferred to the retailer.","Adds shipments to sales, double counting the goods that were sold."],"wt":["trap","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Goods on consignment stay in inventory; revenue is recognized when the consignee sells to the end customer.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Consignment: control does not transfer on shipment to the consignee","Revenue recognized on sale to end customers: 180,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0019","t":"Delta Cloud Inc. pays a sales agent a $60,000 commission for winning a three-year customer contract. The commission is incremental and would not have been paid otherwise. Delta expects the customer to renew, and the commission is not commensurate with the renewal, so the benefit relates to about 5 years of service. What is the 2026 amortization of the commission, assuming the full year applies?","mc":{"s":"Delta Cloud Inc. pays a sales agent a $60,000 commission for winning a three-year customer contract. The commission is incremental and would not have been paid otherwise. Delta expects the customer to renew, and the commission is not commensurate with the renewal, so the benefit relates to about 5 years of service. What is the 2026 amortization of the commission, assuming the full year applies?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$12,000","$20,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 340-40-25-1; ASC 340-40-35-1","rat":"The commission is capitalized because it is incremental and recoverable, and amortized over the expected period of benefit, including anticipated renewals: $60,000 / 5 = $12,000.","ex":["Amortizes over six years, which does not match the expected period of benefit.","The commission is capitalized because it is incremental and recoverable, and amortized over the expected period of benefit, including anticipated renewals: $60,000 / 5 = $12,000.","Amortizes over the initial three-year term only. The period should reflect anticipated renewals when the commission is not commensurate with the renewal.","Expenses the entire commission when incurred. The practical expedient applies only if the amortization period is one year or less."],"wt":["calc","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Incremental costs of obtaining a contract are capitalized and amortized over the period of benefit, including anticipated renewals.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Capitalize the 60,000 commission","Period of benefit including expected renewal 5 years","Annual amortization 60,000 / 5 = 12,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0020","t":"Prism Analytics Inc. pays a $9,000 sales commission on a customer contract with a term of eight months and no renewal expected. How may the commission be treated?","mc":{"s":"Prism Analytics Inc. pays a $9,000 sales commission on a customer contract with a term of eight months and no renewal expected. How may the commission be treated?","d":"","o":["Expensed when incurred, using the practical expedient for a period of one year or less","Capitalized and amortized over the remaining life of the customer relationship regardless of the contract term","Deferred until the customer pays, and then recognized as a reduction of revenue","Capitalized as a contract asset because the costs are directly related to the contract"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 340-40-25-4","rat":"If the amortization period would be one year or less, an entity may expense incremental costs of obtaining a contract when incurred.","ex":["If the amortization period would be one year or less, an entity may expense incremental costs of obtaining a contract when incurred.","Amortization follows the period of benefit. Here the contract term is short and the expedient allows immediate expense.","Commissions paid to an employee or agent are costs, not reductions of the transaction price.","A contract asset is a right to consideration. Costs of obtaining a contract are a different asset, and the expedient permits expense here."],"wt":["","concept","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The practical expedient allows expensing incremental contract acquisition costs when the amortization period is one year or less.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0021","t":"Summit Grocers Inc. sells goods for $100,000 to loyalty program members, who also earn points redeemable for future discounts. The standalone selling price of the points, adjusted for expected redemption, is $5,000. The goods' standalone selling price is $100,000. How much revenue is recognized on the sale of the goods?","mc":{"s":"Summit Grocers Inc. sells goods for $100,000 to loyalty program members, who also earn points redeemable for future discounts. The standalone selling price of the points, adjusted for expected redemption, is $5,000. The goods' standalone selling price is $100,000. How much revenue is recognized on the sale of the goods?","d":"","o":["$5,000","$95,000","$95,238","$100,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-42; ASC 606-10-32-31","rat":"The points are a material right, a separate performance obligation.","ex":["Reports the value of the points as revenue, which reverses the allocation.","Deducts the SSP of the points from the price rather than allocating on a relative SSP basis.","The points are a material right, a separate performance obligation. The price is allocated by relative SSP: $100,000 x $100,000 / $105,000 = $95,238, and $4,762 is deferred.","Recognizes the whole price. Part of the price relates to the points and is deferred until they are redeemed or expire."],"wt":["trap","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A customer option that gives a material right is a separate obligation; allocate the price by relative standalone selling price.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total SSP 100,000 + 5,000 = 105,000","Goods share 100,000 / 105,000 = 95.238%","Revenue 100,000 x 95.238% = 95,238; deferred 4,762"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0022","t":"An entity sells equipment together with installation. The installation is routine, and other vendors could perform it without changing the equipment. When are the equipment and installation separate performance obligations?","mc":{"s":"An entity sells equipment together with installation. The installation is routine, and other vendors could perform it without changing the equipment. When are the equipment and installation separate performance obligations?","d":"","o":["When the equipment and installation are invoiced as separate line items on the same document","When the entity is legally required to deliver each item on a different date","When each is capable of being distinct and is distinct in the context of the contract, so the customer can benefit from each on its own or with readily available resources","When the installation costs less than the equipment, whatever the terms of the contract"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-19; ASC 606-10-25-21","rat":"Goods or services are distinct if capable of being distinct and separately identifiable from other promises in the contract.","ex":["Separate billing does not determine distinctness. The nature of the promises does.","Timing of delivery alone does not make items distinct. The assessment focuses on the customer's ability to benefit and separate identifiability.","Goods or services are distinct if capable of being distinct and separately identifiable from other promises in the contract.","Relative cost is not a distinctness criterion."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A promise is a separate performance obligation if it is capable of being distinct and separately identifiable in the contract.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0023","t":"Radial Tools Inc. contracts to sell goods for $200,000 to a new customer in a country facing a severe currency crisis, and collectibility of the consideration is not probable. The customer prepays a nonrefundable $40,000 and the goods are delivered. No other basis for recognition exists. How does Radial account for the $40,000 received?","mc":{"s":"Radial Tools Inc. contracts to sell goods for $200,000 to a new customer in a country facing a severe currency crisis, and collectibility of the consideration is not probable. The customer prepays a nonrefundable $40,000 and the goods are delivered. No other basis for recognition exists. How does Radial account for the $40,000 received?","d":"","o":["As revenue of $40,000 immediately, because cash has been received and the amount is nonrefundable","As revenue of $200,000 on delivery, with a large allowance for the uncollected amount","As a reduction of the cost of the goods delivered, until collection of the balance becomes probable","As a liability, because the contract criteria are not met and substantially all of the consideration has not been received"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-1; ASC 606-10-25-8","rat":"Without a contract, consideration received is recognized as revenue only when the entity has no remaining obligations and substantially all consideration is received and nonrefundable, or the contract is terminated.","ex":["Cash receipt alone is not enough. Substantially all of the consideration must be received and nonrefundable, or the contract must be terminated.","Without a contract that meets the criteria, revenue is not recognized. An allowance applies only after a contract exists.","Cash received from a customer is consideration, not a reduction of cost of goods.","Without a contract, consideration received is recognized as revenue only when the entity has no remaining obligations and substantially all consideration is received and nonrefundable, or the contract is terminated. Neither has occurred."],"wt":["trap","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If a contract does not meet the criteria, consideration received is a liability until specified conditions are met.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0024","t":"In late December 2026, Glacier Foods Inc. shipped goods with a sales price of $400,000 FOB shipping point on December 28, and $250,000 FOB destination on December 29. The FOB destination goods reached the customer on January 3, 2027. No other sales occurred. How much revenue is recognized in 2026 for these shipments?","mc":{"s":"In late December 2026, Glacier Foods Inc. shipped goods with a sales price of $400,000 FOB shipping point on December 28, and $250,000 FOB destination on December 29. The FOB destination goods reached the customer on January 3, 2027. No other sales occurred. How much revenue is recognized in 2026 for these shipments?","d":"","o":["$150,000","$250,000","$400,000","$650,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-25-30","rat":"Under FOB shipping point, control passes to the customer at shipment (December 28).","ex":["Deducts the FOB destination sale from the shipping point sale, with no basis.","Reverses the terms: control passes at shipment under FOB shipping point, not on arrival.","Under FOB shipping point, control passes to the customer at shipment (December 28). Under FOB destination, control passes on delivery in January 2027.","Includes the FOB destination sale, whose control transfers when the goods arrive in 2027."],"wt":["calc","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Control transfers at shipment under FOB shipping point and on delivery under FOB destination.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["FOB shipping point: control transfers December 28, 2026: 400,000","FOB destination: control transfers January 3, 2027: excluded","2026 revenue 400,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0025","t":"Harvest Beverage Inc. sells drinks to a supermarket chain for $500,000. It also pays the chain a $30,000 slotting fee for premium shelf space. The shelf space is not a distinct service that Harvest could buy from another party. What revenue does Harvest recognize on the sale?","mc":{"s":"Harvest Beverage Inc. sells drinks to a supermarket chain for $500,000. It also pays the chain a $30,000 slotting fee for premium shelf space. The shelf space is not a distinct service that Harvest could buy from another party. What revenue does Harvest recognize on the sale?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$470,000","$500,000","$530,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-25; ASC 606-10-32-26","rat":"Payment to a customer for something that is not distinct is a reduction of the transaction price: $500,000 - $30,000 = $470,000.","ex":["Reports only the fee as revenue.","Payment to a customer for something that is not distinct is a reduction of the transaction price: $500,000 - $30,000 = $470,000.","Recognizes full revenue and expenses the fee. A payment for a non-distinct service reduces the transaction price.","Adds the fee to revenue. Payments to a customer do not increase revenue."],"wt":["trap","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Consideration paid to a customer for non-distinct goods or services reduces the transaction price.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Slotting fee is not for a distinct service","Reduce the transaction price 500,000 - 30,000 = 470,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0026","t":"Quanta Systems Inc. sells hardware, support and a software license for $1,000,000. Hardware sells separately for $300,000 and support for $150,000, but the license price varies widely across customers and has no established price. What amount is allocated to the license using the residual approach?","mc":{"s":"Quanta Systems Inc. sells hardware, support and a software license for $1,000,000. Hardware sells separately for $300,000 and support for $150,000, but the license price varies widely across customers and has no established price. What amount is allocated to the license using the residual approach?","d":"","o":["$550,000","$700,000","$850,000","$1,000,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-34","rat":"When the SSP of a good is highly variable or uncertain, the residual approach may be used: $1,000,000 - $300,000 - $150,000 = $550,000.","ex":["When the SSP of a good is highly variable or uncertain, the residual approach may be used: $1,000,000 - $300,000 - $150,000 = $550,000.","Deducts only the hardware. The support also has an observable SSP and is removed from the residual.","Deducts only support. The hardware also has an observable SSP and is removed from the residual.","Allocates the whole price to the license, ignoring the observable SSPs of the other items."],"wt":["","calc","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The residual approach is allowed when a good's SSP is highly variable or uncertain: price less observable SSPs of other items.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Observable SSPs: hardware 300,000, support 150,000","License = 1,000,000 - 300,000 - 150,000 = 550,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0027","t":"On October 1, 2026, Solstice Learning Inc. sold 600 one-year online subscriptions at $240 each, paid in advance. Service is provided evenly throughout the year. How much revenue is recognized in 2026, and what is reported as unearned revenue at December 31?","mc":{"s":"On October 1, 2026, Solstice Learning Inc. sold 600 one-year online subscriptions at $240 each, paid in advance. Service is provided evenly throughout the year. How much revenue is recognized in 2026, and what is reported as unearned revenue at December 31?","d":"","o":["$144,000 revenue and $0 unearned revenue","$108,000 revenue and $36,000 unearned revenue","$72,000 revenue and $72,000 unearned revenue","$36,000 revenue and $108,000 unearned revenue"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 606-10-45-2; ASC 606-10-25-27","rat":"Three of twelve months are earned: $144,000 x 3/12 = $36,000, and 9/12, or $108,000, remains a contract liability.","ex":["Recognizes the full annual fee at receipt. The service is provided over time, so revenue is recognized ratably.","Swaps the earned and unearned portions.","Uses six months. The subscriptions began October 1, so only three months are earned by year-end.","Three of twelve months are earned: $144,000 x 3/12 = $36,000, and 9/12, or $108,000, remains a contract liability."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Advance receipts for services provided over time are contract liabilities and recognized ratably as service is delivered.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total billings 600 x 240 = 144,000","Earned Oct-Dec: 144,000 x 3/12 = 36,000","Unearned 144,000 - 36,000 = 108,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0028","t":"Ridgeline Equipment Inc. sells a machine together with a three-year extended service warranty for $1,000,000. The machine has a standard warranty that assures conformance to specifications, and the extended warranty is a distinct service. Standalone selling prices are $950,000 for the machine and $150,000 for the extended warranty. Control of the machine transfers at the start of year 1, and the three-year extended warranty coverage also begins on that date. What total revenue is recognized in year 1?","mc":{"s":"Ridgeline Equipment Inc. sells a machine together with a three-year extended service warranty for $1,000,000. The machine has a standard warranty that assures conformance to specifications, and the extended warranty is a distinct service. Standalone selling prices are $950,000 for the machine and $150,000 for the extended warranty. Control of the machine transfers at the start of year 1, and the three-year extended warranty coverage also begins on that date. What total revenue is recognized in year 1?","d":"","o":["$863,636","$909,091","$950,000","$1,000,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-30; ASC 606-10-55-33","rat":"Price is allocated $863,636 to the machine and $136,364 to the service warranty.","ex":["Recognizes only the machine's allocated price and omits the first-year revenue from the extended warranty.","Price is allocated $863,636 to the machine and $136,364 to the service warranty. The machine is recognized at delivery and the warranty over three years ($45,455 per year): $909,091.","Recognizes the machine at its standalone selling price and omits the warranty revenue, instead of allocating the actual $1,000,000 price.","Recognizes the entire price at delivery. The extended warranty is a service obligation satisfied over three years."],"wt":["calc","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A distinct extended warranty is a separate obligation recognized over its term; the standard warranty is an assurance-type cost accrual.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Machine allocation 1,000,000 x 950,000 / 1,100,000 = 863,636; warranty allocation 136,364","Warranty revenue 136,364 / 3 = 45,455 in year 1","Total year 1 revenue 863,636 + 45,455 = 909,091"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0029","t":"Zenith Parts Inc. sells 1,000 units to a customer during the first quarter at a list price of $100. The contract gives a retrospective 5% discount on all units purchased in the year if annual purchases exceed 10,000 units. Zenith estimates, based on reliable data, that the customer will exceed the threshold. What revenue does Zenith recognize on the first-quarter sales?","mc":{"s":"Zenith Parts Inc. sells 1,000 units to a customer during the first quarter at a list price of $100. The contract gives a retrospective 5% discount on all units purchased in the year if annual purchases exceed 10,000 units. Zenith estimates, based on reliable data, that the customer will exceed the threshold. What revenue does Zenith recognize on the first-quarter sales?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$95,000","$100,000","$105,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-32-5; ASC 606-10-55-216","rat":"Because the discount is expected to be earned, the transaction price reflects it from the first sale: 1,000 x $100 x 95% = $95,000, with a refund liability of $5,000.","ex":["Applies a discount of twice the contractual rate.","Because the discount is expected to be earned, the transaction price reflects it from the first sale: 1,000 x $100 x 95% = $95,000, with a refund liability of $5,000.","Recognizes list price and ignores the expected retrospective discount, which reduces the transaction price.","Adds the discount to revenue instead of deducting it."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Expected volume discounts are variable consideration that reduces the transaction price from the first sale.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["List price 1,000 x 100 = 100,000","Expected retrospective discount 5% x 100,000 = 5,000 (refund liability)","Revenue 100,000 - 5,000 = 95,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0030","t":"Aldrin Interiors Inc. contracts to deliver two units, each for $50,000, with payment for the first unit conditional on delivery of the second. At December 31, 2026, Aldrin has delivered only the first unit, which is a distinct performance obligation. How is the $50,000 recorded?","mc":{"s":"Aldrin Interiors Inc. contracts to deliver two units, each for $50,000, with payment for the first unit conditional on delivery of the second. At December 31, 2026, Aldrin has delivered only the first unit, which is a distinct performance obligation. How is the $50,000 recorded?","d":"","o":["As revenue and a receivable, because the first unit has been delivered and control has transferred to the customer","As unearned revenue, because Aldrin has received no cash for the delivered unit","As revenue and a contract asset, because the right to payment depends on something other than the passage of time","As deferred revenue until both units are delivered, because the units are bundled in one contract"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 606-10-45-3; ASC 606-10-45-4","rat":"A right to consideration that is conditional on future performance is a contract asset.","ex":["A receivable requires an unconditional right to consideration. Here payment depends on delivering the second unit.","A contract liability arises when the customer pays first or payment is due before performance, which is not the case.","A right to consideration that is conditional on future performance is a contract asset. It becomes a receivable when only the passage of time is required.","Because the first unit is a distinct obligation, revenue is recognized when it transfers, not at the end of the contract."],"wt":["trap","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A conditional right to payment is a contract asset; it becomes a receivable when only time must pass.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0031","t":"Corridor Fitness Inc. enrolls 400 members in twelve-month contracts, each paying a nonrefundable $300 joining fee at signing and $50 per month. The joining fee does not transfer a separate service. All contracts started January 1, 2026, with the same term. What total revenue does Corridor recognize in the first three months?","mc":{"s":"Corridor Fitness Inc. enrolls 400 members in twelve-month contracts, each paying a nonrefundable $300 joining fee at signing and $50 per month. The joining fee does not transfer a separate service. All contracts started January 1, 2026, with the same term. What total revenue does Corridor recognize in the first three months?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$90,000","$180,000","$360,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-50; ASC 606-10-55-51","rat":"The joining fee is part of the transaction price for the membership, so total consideration of $900 per member is recognized evenly: $75 per month x 3 x 400 = $90,000.","ex":["Recognizes only the monthly dues and defers the fee until the end of the contract.","The joining fee is part of the transaction price for the membership, so total consideration of $900 per member is recognized evenly: $75 per month x 3 x 400 = $90,000.","Recognizes the entire joining fee upfront plus three months of dues. The fee does not relate to a separate service.","Recognizes all consideration in the first three months."],"wt":["trap","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A nonrefundable upfront fee that relates to no separate service is recognized over the contract period with the other consideration.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total consideration per member 300 + 50 x 12 = 900","Monthly revenue 900 / 12 = 75","Three months x 400 members x 75 = 90,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-C-0032","t":"Which factor best indicates that an entity is a principal, rather than an agent, in providing goods to a customer through a third-party supplier?","mc":{"s":"Which factor best indicates that an entity is a principal, rather than an agent, in providing goods to a customer through a third-party supplier?","d":"","o":["The entity controls the specified good or service before it is transferred to the customer, including primary responsibility for fulfilment","The entity earns a fixed commission that does not vary with the price the customer pays","The entity does not take title to the goods, since the supplier ships directly to the customer","The entity's customer has the right to return unwanted goods to the supplier directly"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 606-10-55-36; ASC 606-10-55-39","rat":"Principal status depends on control of the specified good or service before transfer.","ex":["Principal status depends on control of the specified good or service before transfer. Responsibility for fulfilment, inventory risk and pricing discretion are indicators.","A fixed commission is an indicator of agent status, not principal.","Lack of title or physical possession alone does not decide the question, but it points toward agent status when combined with no control.","Return rights against the supplier are not an indicator of principal status."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A principal controls the specified good or service before it transfers to the customer.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"4":[{"i":"FAR-III-D-0001","t":"Which item creates a permanent difference between pretax financial income and taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Which item creates a permanent difference between pretax financial income and taxable income?","d":"","o":["Depreciation computed by an accelerated method for tax and straight-line for financial reporting","Warranty costs accrued for financial reporting but deductible for tax when paid","Interest income received on municipal bonds that is exempt from federal income tax","Revenue recognized in the current year for financial reporting but taxed when collected in a later year"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-20","rat":"Tax-exempt interest is included in book income and never in taxable income, so the difference never reverses and creates no deferred tax.","ex":["This is a temporary difference. It reverses over the asset's life and creates a deferred tax liability.","This is a temporary difference that reverses when the warranty is paid, creating a deferred tax asset.","Tax-exempt interest is included in book income and never in taxable income, so the difference never reverses and creates no deferred tax.","This is a temporary difference that reverses upon collection, creating a deferred tax liability."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Permanent differences never reverse and create no deferred taxes; temporary differences reverse and create deferred taxes.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0002","t":"Cascade Manufacturing Inc. reports 2026 pretax financial income of $600,000. It includes $40,000 of tax-exempt municipal interest. Tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $90,000, and warranty expense recorded for books exceeds warranty payments deducted for tax by $20,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is 2026 current income tax expense?","mc":{"s":"Cascade Manufacturing Inc. reports 2026 pretax financial income of $600,000. It includes $40,000 of tax-exempt municipal interest. Tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $90,000, and warranty expense recorded for books exceeds warranty payments deducted for tax by $20,000. The tax rate is 25%. What is 2026 current income tax expense?","d":"","o":["$112,500","$122,500","$132,500","$142,500"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-2","rat":"Taxable income is $600,000 - $40,000 - $90,000 + $20,000 = $490,000, and 25% of that is $122,500.","ex":["Subtracts the warranty difference. Book expense exceeds tax deductions, so the difference is added back.","Taxable income is $600,000 - $40,000 - $90,000 + $20,000 = $490,000, and 25% of that is $122,500.","Leaves the tax-exempt interest in taxable income. Municipal interest is deducted to arrive at taxable income.","Adds the tax-exempt interest instead of removing it."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Taxable income equals pretax book income adjusted for permanent and temporary differences; current tax is taxable income times the rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Pretax book income 600,000","Less exempt interest 40,000 and excess tax depreciation 90,000; add back excess warranty expense 20,000","Taxable income 490,000 x 25% = 122,500"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0003","t":"Using the same facts as a company with pretax financial income of $600,000, $40,000 of exempt municipal interest, a $90,000 excess of tax over book depreciation (temporary), and a $20,000 excess of book warranty expense over deductible payments (temporary), at a 25% tax rate, what is total income tax expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Using the same facts as a company with pretax financial income of $600,000, $40,000 of exempt municipal interest, a $90,000 excess of tax over book depreciation (temporary), and a $20,000 excess of book warranty expense over deductible payments (temporary), at a 25% tax rate, what is total income tax expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$122,500","$140,000","$145,000","$150,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-5; ASC 740-10-45-2","rat":"Current tax $122,500 + deferred tax expense ($22,500 DTL increase - $5,000 DTA increase = $17,500) = $140,000, which equals ($600,000 - $40,000) x 25%.","ex":["Reports only current tax. Total expense includes deferred tax expense from the changes in DTL and DTA.","Current tax $122,500 + deferred tax expense ($22,500 DTL increase - $5,000 DTA increase = $17,500) = $140,000, which equals ($600,000 - $40,000) x 25%.","Adds the DTL increase but ignores the deferred tax asset created by the warranty difference.","Adds the DTA increase instead of subtracting it. A DTA increase reduces deferred tax expense."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Total income tax expense equals current tax plus deferred tax expense (increase in net DTL less increase in DTA).","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Current tax (600,000 - 40,000 - 90,000 + 20,000) x 25% = 122,500","Deferred: DTL 90,000 x 25% = 22,500 increase; DTA 20,000 x 25% = 5,000 increase; net 17,500 expense","Total 122,500 + 17,500 = 140,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0004","t":"At December 31, 2026, Marina Shipping Inc. has equipment with a book carrying amount of $800,000 and a tax basis of $500,000. The enacted tax rate is 30%, and no other differences exist. What deferred tax amount is recorded at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2026, Marina Shipping Inc. has equipment with a book carrying amount of $800,000 and a tax basis of $500,000. The enacted tax rate is 30%, and no other differences exist. What deferred tax amount is recorded at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$90,000 deferred tax asset","$240,000 deferred tax liability","$150,000 deferred tax liability","$90,000 deferred tax liability"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-30-8","rat":"The carrying amount exceeds the tax basis by $300,000, a taxable temporary difference: $300,000 x 30% = $90,000.","ex":["Same amount, wrong nature. Recovering a book amount above tax basis produces taxable income later, which is a liability.","Applies the rate to the full carrying amount rather than the temporary difference.","Applies the rate to the tax basis rather than to the difference between carrying amount and tax basis.","The carrying amount exceeds the tax basis by $300,000, a taxable temporary difference: $300,000 x 30% = $90,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deferred tax equals the enacted rate times the temporary difference; carrying amount above tax basis gives a liability.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Temporary difference 800,000 - 500,000 = 300,000 (taxable)","DTL 300,000 x 30% = 90,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0005","t":"At December 31, 2025, Pelican Aviation Inc. had a deferred tax liability of $200,000, computed as a 25% rate on $800,000 of taxable temporary differences that will reverse in 2028 and later. In 2026, legislation is enacted that lowers the rate to 21% for years beginning after 2026. No other changes in the differences occurred. What is the effect of the rate change on 2026 income tax expense?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2025, Pelican Aviation Inc. had a deferred tax liability of $200,000, computed as a 25% rate on $800,000 of taxable temporary differences that will reverse in 2028 and later. In 2026, legislation is enacted that lowers the rate to 21% for years beginning after 2026. No other changes in the differences occurred. What is the effect of the rate change on 2026 income tax expense?","d":"","o":["$32,000 decrease, recognized in income from continuing operations in 2026","$32,000 decrease, recognized directly in retained earnings as a change in principle","$168,000 decrease, because the liability is remeasured at the new rate","No effect until the lower rate begins to apply to taxable income in the year after 2026"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-10-35-4; ASC 740-10-45-15","rat":"The DTL is remeasured to $168,000, and the $32,000 adjustment is recognized in income tax expense from continuing operations in the period of enactment.","ex":["The DTL is remeasured to $168,000, and the $32,000 adjustment is recognized in income tax expense from continuing operations in the period of enactment.","Right amount, wrong reporting. A tax rate change is recognized in income from continuing operations in the enactment period, not retrospectively.","Treats the new liability balance as the adjustment. The effect is the change in the liability, not its ending balance.","Deferred taxes are remeasured on the enactment date, not when the new rate takes effect."],"wt":["","concept","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Effects of an enacted rate change on deferred taxes are recognized in continuing operations in the period of enactment.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Old DTL 800,000 x 25% = 200,000","Remeasured DTL 800,000 x 21% = 168,000","Decrease in tax expense 200,000 - 168,000 = 32,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0006","t":"Crestline Retail Inc. has deductible temporary differences that produced a deferred tax asset of $300,000. Based on projections of taxable income and available tax planning strategies, management concludes it is more likely than not that 40% of the deferred tax asset will not be realized. What valuation allowance is required?","mc":{"s":"Crestline Retail Inc. has deductible temporary differences that produced a deferred tax asset of $300,000. Based on projections of taxable income and available tax planning strategies, management concludes it is more likely than not that 40% of the deferred tax asset will not be realized. What valuation allowance is required?","d":"","o":["$120,000","$150,000","$180,000","$300,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-5; ASC 740-10-30-17","rat":"A valuation allowance reduces the deferred tax asset by the portion that is more likely than not to be unrealized: $300,000 x 40% = $120,000.","ex":["A valuation allowance reduces the deferred tax asset by the portion that is more likely than not to be unrealized: $300,000 x 40% = $120,000.","Applies a 50% threshold rate to the asset. The allowance equals the portion expected not to be realized, not the threshold probability.","Uses the expected realized portion as the allowance. The allowance covers the portion that will not be realized.","Fully reserves the asset. A full allowance applies only if it is more likely than not that none of it will be realized."],"wt":["","trap","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A valuation allowance is recorded for the portion of a deferred tax asset that is more likely than not to be unrealized.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Portion more likely than not unrealized 40%","Valuation allowance 300,000 x 40% = 120,000; net DTA 180,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0007","t":"Ivory Digital Inc. has a 2026 tax loss of $400,000 that it carries forward indefinitely. The enacted tax rate is 21%, and management believes it is more likely than not that the loss will be fully used against future taxable income. What deferred tax amount is recognized?","mc":{"s":"Ivory Digital Inc. has a 2026 tax loss of $400,000 that it carries forward indefinitely. The enacted tax rate is 21%, and management believes it is more likely than not that the loss will be fully used against future taxable income. What deferred tax amount is recognized?","d":"","o":["$400,000 deferred tax asset, with no valuation allowance","$84,000 deferred tax liability, because the loss will reduce future tax","No deferred tax asset, because tax benefits are recognized only when a refund is received","$84,000 deferred tax asset, with no valuation allowance"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-3; ASC 740-10-30-5","rat":"An NOL carryforward gives rise to a deferred tax asset equal to the loss times the enacted rate: $400,000 x 21% = $84,000.","ex":["Recognizes the gross loss rather than the tax effect.","The loss carryforward creates a future deduction, which is a deferred tax asset, not a liability.","A carryforward benefit is recognized as a deferred tax asset, subject to a valuation allowance test.","An NOL carryforward gives rise to a deferred tax asset equal to the loss times the enacted rate: $400,000 x 21% = $84,000."],"wt":["calc","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A net operating loss carryforward creates a deferred tax asset measured at the enacted rate, reduced by any needed allowance.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["NOL 400,000 x 21% = 84,000","Realization more likely than not: no valuation allowance"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0008","t":"How does a company classify deferred tax assets and liabilities in a classified balance sheet?","mc":{"s":"How does a company classify deferred tax assets and liabilities in a classified balance sheet?","d":"","o":["As current or noncurrent according to the classification of the asset or liability that gave rise to the difference","As current if expected to reverse within 12 months and noncurrent otherwise","As noncurrent, with assets and liabilities offset by tax-paying component and jurisdiction","As noncurrent, but assets and liabilities are presented gross, without any offset"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-45-4; ASC 740-10-45-6","rat":"All deferred tax assets and liabilities are classified as noncurrent, and are netted within each tax-paying component of an entity and jurisdiction.","ex":["This was the older approach. Current guidance classifies all deferred taxes as noncurrent.","Expected reversal timing no longer determines classification.","All deferred tax assets and liabilities are classified as noncurrent, and are netted within each tax-paying component of an entity and jurisdiction.","Offsetting is required within a tax-paying component of an entity and a tax jurisdiction."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"All deferred tax assets and liabilities are noncurrent, netted within each tax-paying component and jurisdiction.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0009","t":"Talon Logistics Inc. claimed a $100,000 tax benefit on a return, and it is more likely than not that the position will be sustained on its technical merits. Management estimates the outcomes on examination: $100,000 benefit sustained, 30% likely; $80,000 benefit, 25% likely; $40,000 benefit, 25% likely; $0 benefit, 20% likely. What liability for the unrecognized tax benefit is recorded?","mc":{"s":"Talon Logistics Inc. claimed a $100,000 tax benefit on a return, and it is more likely than not that the position will be sustained on its technical merits. Management estimates the outcomes on examination: $100,000 benefit sustained, 30% likely; $80,000 benefit, 25% likely; $40,000 benefit, 25% likely; $0 benefit, 20% likely. What liability for the unrecognized tax benefit is recorded?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$40,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-7; ASC 740-10-25-6","rat":"The largest benefit with a cumulative likelihood above 50% is $80,000 (30% + 25% = 55%).","ex":["Assumes the whole benefit is recognized because the recognition threshold is met. Measurement then limits the recognized amount to $80,000.","The largest benefit with a cumulative likelihood above 50% is $80,000 (30% + 25% = 55%). The unrecognized benefit is $100,000 - $80,000 = $20,000.","Uses the probability-weighted benefit of $60,000 (100,000 - 60,000). The measurement is the largest amount that is more than 50% likely, not an expected value.","Treats the entire claimed benefit as unrecognized, even though most of it meets the recognition and measurement rules."],"wt":["concept","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Measure the recognized benefit at the largest amount that is greater than 50 percent likely to be realized on settlement.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cumulative likelihood: 100,000 at 30%; 80,000 or more at 55%","Largest amount above 50% is 80,000","Unrecognized benefit (liability) 100,000 - 80,000 = 20,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0010","t":"Vector Instruments Inc. has $500,000 of pretax income from continuing operations and a $100,000 pretax loss from discontinued operations, both before tax, in 2026. The tax rate is 30%. What is the income tax expense on continuing operations?","mc":{"s":"Vector Instruments Inc. has $500,000 of pretax income from continuing operations and a $100,000 pretax loss from discontinued operations, both before tax, in 2026. The tax rate is 30%. What is the income tax expense on continuing operations?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$120,000","$150,000","$180,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-20-45-2","rat":"Tax is allocated to each component: continuing operations $500,000 x 30% = $150,000, and the discontinued loss produces a $30,000 tax benefit.","ex":["Reports the tax benefit on the discontinued operations.","Uses total pretax income of $400,000. That amount is the total tax, not the tax allocated to continuing operations.","Tax is allocated to each component: continuing operations $500,000 x 30% = $150,000, and the discontinued loss produces a $30,000 tax benefit.","Adds the discontinued loss to continuing income instead of allocating tax separately."],"wt":["calc","trap","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income tax is allocated to continuing operations and other items separately; each component carries its own tax effect.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Continuing operations 500,000 x 30% = 150,000 tax expense","Discontinued operations loss 100,000 x 30% = 30,000 tax benefit","Total tax 120,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0011","t":"Palisade Holdings Inc. had a deferred tax liability of $60,000 at January 1, 2026, and, after 2026 activity, a taxable temporary difference of $400,000 at December 31, 2026, taxed at 25%. Current income tax payable for 2026 is $90,000. What is total income tax expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Palisade Holdings Inc. had a deferred tax liability of $60,000 at January 1, 2026, and, after 2026 activity, a taxable temporary difference of $400,000 at December 31, 2026, taxed at 25%. Current income tax payable for 2026 is $90,000. What is total income tax expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$130,000","$150,000","$190,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-5; ASC 740-10-45-2","rat":"The DTL at year-end is $100,000, so deferred tax expense is $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000.","ex":["Reports current tax only and omits the deferred component.","The DTL at year-end is $100,000, so deferred tax expense is $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000. Total expense is $90,000 + $40,000 = $130,000.","Adds the opening DTL, which does not measure the change during the year.","Adds the entire year-end DTL instead of the change in the DTL during the year."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deferred tax expense equals the change in net deferred tax liability during the year; total expense adds current tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Ending DTL 400,000 x 25% = 100,000","Deferred tax expense 100,000 - 60,000 = 40,000","Total 90,000 + 40,000 = 130,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0012","t":"In December 2026, Timber Storage Inc. received $120,000 of rent for January through December 2027. The rent is taxable when received but recognized as revenue for books in 2027. The tax rate is 25%. What deferred tax amount does Timber record at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"In December 2026, Timber Storage Inc. received $120,000 of rent for January through December 2027. The rent is taxable when received but recognized as revenue for books in 2027. The tax rate is 25%. What deferred tax amount does Timber record at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$30,000 deferred tax asset","$30,000 deferred tax liability","$120,000 deferred tax asset","No deferred tax, because the difference arises in one period and reverses in the next"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-55-3","rat":"Book revenue is deferred while taxable income is recognized now, giving a deductible temporary difference of $120,000 and a DTA of $30,000.","ex":["Book revenue is deferred while taxable income is recognized now, giving a deductible temporary difference of $120,000 and a DTA of $30,000.","Same amount, wrong direction. The rent was taxed earlier than it is recognized in book income, which creates a DTA.","Records the temporary difference itself, not the tax effect at 25%.","Any temporary difference that reverses in a later period creates deferred tax, regardless of how quickly it reverses."],"wt":["","concept","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income taxed before it is recognized for books creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable in 2026, recognized for books in 2027: deductible temporary difference 120,000","DTA 120,000 x 25% = 30,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0013","t":"Sunrise Land Corp. sold land in 2026 and recognized a $500,000 gross profit for financial reporting immediately. For tax, it uses the installment method, and 30% of the sale price is collected in 2026, with the remainder collected in later years. The tax rate is 20%. What deferred tax liability is recorded at year-end?","mc":{"s":"Sunrise Land Corp. sold land in 2026 and recognized a $500,000 gross profit for financial reporting immediately. For tax, it uses the installment method, and 30% of the sale price is collected in 2026, with the remainder collected in later years. The tax rate is 20%. What deferred tax liability is recorded at year-end?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$70,000","$100,000","$350,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-55-3","rat":"Taxable gross profit in 2026 is $150,000, so $350,000 of book gross profit is not yet taxed: $350,000 x 20% = $70,000.","ex":["Uses the portion already taxed instead of the untaxed portion.","Taxable gross profit in 2026 is $150,000, so $350,000 of book gross profit is not yet taxed: $350,000 x 20% = $70,000.","Applies the tax rate to the full gross profit. Part of the profit was taxed in 2026.","Reports the temporary difference without applying the tax rate."],"wt":["calc","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Gross profit recognized for books but deferred for tax creates a taxable temporary difference and a deferred tax liability.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book gross profit 500,000; taxable in 2026 500,000 x 30% = 150,000","Temporary difference 350,000","DTL 350,000 x 20% = 70,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0014","t":"Vantage Health Inc. reports pretax financial income of $800,000 for 2026. That figure is after a nondeductible regulatory fine of $50,000 and includes $60,000 of tax-exempt interest. There are no temporary differences, and the statutory rate is 21%. What is 2026 income tax expense?","mc":{"s":"Vantage Health Inc. reports pretax financial income of $800,000 for 2026. That figure is after a nondeductible regulatory fine of $50,000 and includes $60,000 of tax-exempt interest. There are no temporary differences, and the statutory rate is 21%. What is 2026 income tax expense?","d":"","o":["$144,900","$165,900","$168,000","$191,100"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-2","rat":"Taxable income is $800,000 + $50,000 fine - $60,000 exempt interest = $790,000, and 21% of that is $165,900.","ex":["Subtracts the nondeductible fine. A nondeductible expense is added back to taxable income.","Taxable income is $800,000 + $50,000 fine - $60,000 exempt interest = $790,000, and 21% of that is $165,900.","Applies the rate to pretax income, ignoring both permanent differences.","Adds back the tax-exempt interest. Exempt income is subtracted to reach taxable income."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Add back nondeductible expenses and subtract exempt income to get taxable income; with no temporary differences, tax expense equals current tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Add back nondeductible fine 50,000","Subtract exempt interest 60,000","Taxable income 790,000 x 21% = 165,900"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0015","t":"Using facts of a company with pretax financial income of $800,000, a $50,000 nondeductible fine, $60,000 of exempt interest and a 21% statutory rate, with no temporary differences, what is the effective tax rate for 2026 (to one decimal)?","mc":{"s":"Using facts of a company with pretax financial income of $800,000, a $50,000 nondeductible fine, $60,000 of exempt interest and a 21% statutory rate, with no temporary differences, what is the effective tax rate for 2026 (to one decimal)?","d":"","o":["18.1%","19.4%","20.7%","21.0%"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-50-12","rat":"Tax expense is $790,000 x 21% = $165,900, and $165,900 / $800,000 = 20.7%.","ex":["Subtracts both the fine and the exempt interest when computing taxable income, instead of adding back the fine.","Removes only the exempt interest ($740,000 x 21% / $800,000) and ignores the nondeductible fine.","Tax expense is $790,000 x 21% = $165,900, and $165,900 / $800,000 = 20.7%.","Reports the statutory rate. The permanent differences change the effective rate."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Effective tax rate is total tax expense divided by pretax income; permanent differences move it away from the statutory rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable income 800,000 + 50,000 - 60,000 = 790,000","Tax 790,000 x 21% = 165,900","Effective rate 165,900 / 800,000 = 20.74%"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0016","t":"Ridge Capital Inc. holds available-for-sale debt securities with an unrealized loss of $50,000 in 2026 that is reported in other comprehensive income. The tax rate is 25%, and the related deferred tax asset is realizable. What amount of unrealized loss is reported in other comprehensive income, net of tax?","mc":{"s":"Ridge Capital Inc. holds available-for-sale debt securities with an unrealized loss of $50,000 in 2026 that is reported in other comprehensive income. The tax rate is 25%, and the related deferred tax asset is realizable. What amount of unrealized loss is reported in other comprehensive income, net of tax?","d":"","o":["$12,500","$37,500","$50,000","$62,500"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-20-45-11; ASC 320-10-35-1","rat":"The tax effect of the loss is recorded in OCI as well, so the net OCI loss is $50,000 - $12,500 = $37,500.","ex":["Reports only the tax effect.","The tax effect of the loss is recorded in OCI as well, so the net OCI loss is $50,000 - $12,500 = $37,500.","Reports the loss before tax. The deferred tax benefit is also presented in other comprehensive income.","Adds the tax effect to the loss, reversing the direction of the tax benefit."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax on items recognized in OCI is allocated to OCI, so the item is reported net of tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Unrealized loss 50,000","Deferred tax benefit 50,000 x 25% = 12,500 recorded in OCI","Net OCI loss 37,500"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0017","t":"Willow Robotics Inc. had a $200,000 valuation allowance against its deferred tax assets at December 31, 2025. In 2026, after sustained profits, management concludes that it is more likely than not that all deferred tax assets will be realized and releases the entire allowance. Pretax income for 2026 is $500,000, current tax is at 20% with no temporary differences arising in 2026. What is 2026 income tax expense (benefit)?","mc":{"s":"Willow Robotics Inc. had a $200,000 valuation allowance against its deferred tax assets at December 31, 2025. In 2026, after sustained profits, management concludes that it is more likely than not that all deferred tax assets will be realized and releases the entire allowance. Pretax income for 2026 is $500,000, current tax is at 20% with no temporary differences arising in 2026. What is 2026 income tax expense (benefit)?","d":"","o":["$100,000 benefit","$100,000 expense","$300,000 expense","$200,000 benefit"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-10-45-20; ASC 740-10-30-5","rat":"Current tax expense of $100,000 is offset by the $200,000 release of the allowance, which is recorded as a tax benefit in continuing operations: a $100,000 net benefit.","ex":["Current tax expense of $100,000 is offset by the $200,000 release of the allowance, which is recorded as a tax benefit in continuing operations: a $100,000 net benefit.","Ignores the release of the valuation allowance. The change in allowance flows through income tax expense.","Adds the release to tax expense instead of deducting it. A release of the allowance reduces expense.","Reports only the release and omits the current tax on 2026 income."],"wt":["","concept","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Changes in a valuation allowance are recognized in income tax expense, so a release lowers expense.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Current tax 500,000 x 20% = 100,000 expense","Release of allowance = 200,000 benefit","Net = 100,000 benefit"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0018","t":"At the end of 2026, a company has taxable temporary differences that will reverse in 2028. Tax legislation that would raise the rate has been proposed but not passed. Which rate is used to measure the deferred tax liability?","mc":{"s":"At the end of 2026, a company has taxable temporary differences that will reverse in 2028. Tax legislation that would raise the rate has been proposed but not passed. Which rate is used to measure the deferred tax liability?","d":"","o":["The proposed higher rate, because it is the expected rate in the year of reversal","The current-year average effective tax rate reported in the income statement","The rate in effect when the temporary difference originated, held constant through reversal, whatever legislation is enacted later","The enacted rate expected to apply to taxable income in the year of reversal, using currently enacted law"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-8","rat":"Deferred taxes use enacted tax rates expected to apply when the differences reverse.","ex":["Proposed but unenacted legislation cannot be anticipated.","The average effective rate reflects permanent items and is not the enacted marginal rate.","Deferred taxes are remeasured for changes in enacted rates; the origination rate is not locked in.","Deferred taxes use enacted tax rates expected to apply when the differences reverse. Proposed changes are ignored until enactment."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deferred taxes use the enacted tax rate expected to apply in the years the temporary differences reverse.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0019","t":"Basalt Energy Inc. has cumulative pretax losses over the last three years, and a deferred tax asset of $150,000 from deductible temporary differences that will reverse in 2027 and 2028. It also has a deferred tax liability of $220,000 from taxable temporary differences that will reverse in the same years and the same jurisdiction. It has no other sources of taxable income. What valuation allowance is required for the deferred tax asset?","mc":{"s":"Basalt Energy Inc. has cumulative pretax losses over the last three years, and a deferred tax asset of $150,000 from deductible temporary differences that will reverse in 2027 and 2028. It also has a deferred tax liability of $220,000 from taxable temporary differences that will reverse in the same years and the same jurisdiction. It has no other sources of taxable income. What valuation allowance is required for the deferred tax asset?","d":"","o":["The full $150,000, because cumulative losses in recent years rule out any other evidence","$70,000, the excess of the deferred tax liability over the deferred tax asset","None, because the reversal of the taxable temporary differences in the same periods provides enough taxable income to realize the asset","$75,000, half of the asset, because evidence is mixed between losses and reversals"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-18; ASC 740-10-30-21","rat":"Future reversals of existing taxable temporary differences are a source of taxable income of the appropriate character and period, which supports realization despite cumulative losses.","ex":["Cumulative losses are negative evidence but do not override reversing taxable temporary differences, which are objectively verifiable positive evidence.","The excess of the DTL over the DTA does not measure any shortfall. The DTA is fully supported by the DTL.","Future reversals of existing taxable temporary differences are a source of taxable income of the appropriate character and period, which supports realization despite cumulative losses.","The allowance is not set by splitting mixed evidence. It covers the portion that is more likely than not unrealized."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Reversals of existing taxable temporary differences in the same periods are a source of taxable income supporting realization.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0020","t":"Halden Group Inc. has a foreign subsidiary with $4,000,000 of undistributed earnings. Management has specific plans to reinvest these earnings indefinitely outside the United States, and intends not to repatriate them. How does the parent account for the outside basis difference for income taxes?","mc":{"s":"Halden Group Inc. has a foreign subsidiary with $4,000,000 of undistributed earnings. Management has specific plans to reinvest these earnings indefinitely outside the United States, and intends not to repatriate them. How does the parent account for the outside basis difference for income taxes?","d":"","o":["It does not record a deferred tax liability on the undistributed earnings, but discloses the amount of the unrecognized liability if practicable","It records a deferred tax liability at the U.S. rate because the earnings are included in consolidated income","It records the liability only when the earnings are actually distributed as dividends","It records a valuation allowance against the entire $4,000,000 of undistributed earnings"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-30-25-17; ASC 740-30-50-2","rat":"The indefinite reversal exception excludes outside basis differences in foreign subsidiaries when earnings are indefinitely reinvested, subject to disclosure.","ex":["The indefinite reversal exception excludes outside basis differences in foreign subsidiaries when earnings are indefinitely reinvested, subject to disclosure.","Recording the liability is required only if the exception does not apply, for example when repatriation is planned.","Recognition when distributed applies to current tax. The exception itself rests on management's indefinite reinvestment intent.","A valuation allowance applies to deferred tax assets, not to undistributed earnings."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A deferred tax liability is not recognized for foreign subsidiary earnings that are indefinitely reinvested; disclosure is required.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0021","t":"In 2026, Meridian Textiles Inc. reports pretax financial income of $700,000. Tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $200,000, and bad debt expense for books exceeds write-offs deducted for tax by $50,000. No permanent differences exist, and the tax rate is 30%. What is deferred income tax expense for 2026?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Meridian Textiles Inc. reports pretax financial income of $700,000. Tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $200,000, and bad debt expense for books exceeds write-offs deducted for tax by $50,000. No permanent differences exist, and the tax rate is 30%. What is deferred income tax expense for 2026?","d":"","o":["$15,000","$45,000","$60,000","$75,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-45-2; ASC 740-10-30-5","rat":"The depreciation difference adds $60,000 to the deferred tax liability, and the bad debt difference adds $15,000 to the deferred tax asset.","ex":["Uses only the deferred tax asset increase.","The depreciation difference adds $60,000 to the deferred tax liability, and the bad debt difference adds $15,000 to the deferred tax asset. Net deferred tax expense is $45,000.","Ignores the deferred tax asset from the bad debt difference.","Adds both amounts. An increase in a deferred tax asset reduces deferred tax expense."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deferred tax expense equals the net increase in deferred tax liabilities less the net increase in deferred tax assets.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["DTL increase 200,000 x 30% = 60,000","DTA increase 50,000 x 30% = 15,000","Deferred tax expense 60,000 - 15,000 = 45,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0022","t":"At December 31, 2025, Northgate Freight Inc. had taxable temporary differences of $500,000 and deductible temporary differences of $200,000, both measured at 30%. In 2026, a lower rate of 25% is enacted for all future years. There were no other changes in the differences. What is the effect of the rate change on 2026 income tax expense?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2025, Northgate Freight Inc. had taxable temporary differences of $500,000 and deductible temporary differences of $200,000, both measured at 30%. In 2026, a lower rate of 25% is enacted for all future years. There were no other changes in the differences. What is the effect of the rate change on 2026 income tax expense?","d":"","o":["$25,000 decrease","$10,000 decrease","$35,000 decrease","$15,000 decrease"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 740-10-35-4","rat":"The net taxable difference of $300,000 times the 5-point rate reduction gives $15,000: the DTL falls $25,000 and the DTA falls $10,000, a net $15,000 decrease in tax expense.","ex":["Reflects only the reduction in the deferred tax liability ($25,000) and ignores the reduction of the deferred tax asset.","Reflects only the reduction in the deferred tax asset ($10,000) and ignores the deferred tax liability.","Adds the two differences. The DTA reduction offsets the DTL reduction, so the net effect is smaller.","The net taxable difference of $300,000 times the 5-point rate reduction gives $15,000: the DTL falls $25,000 and the DTA falls $10,000, a net $15,000 decrease in tax expense."],"wt":["calc","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A rate change remeasures both deferred tax assets and liabilities; the net adjustment goes through income tax expense.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["DTL falls 500,000 x 5% = 25,000 (tax benefit)","DTA falls 200,000 x 5% = 10,000 (tax expense)","Net decrease in tax expense 25,000 - 10,000 = 15,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0023","t":"Quarry Materials Inc. has a $250,000 net operating loss carryforward and a tax rate of 21%. Based on available evidence, it is more likely than not that 60% of the resulting deferred tax asset will not be realized. What is the net deferred tax asset reported?","mc":{"s":"Quarry Materials Inc. has a $250,000 net operating loss carryforward and a tax rate of 21%. Based on available evidence, it is more likely than not that 60% of the resulting deferred tax asset will not be realized. What is the net deferred tax asset reported?","d":"","o":["$21,000","$31,500","$52,500","$100,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-5; ASC 740-10-30-17","rat":"The gross DTA is $250,000 x 21% = $52,500.","ex":["The gross DTA is $250,000 x 21% = $52,500. A valuation allowance of $31,500 (60%) leaves a net deferred tax asset of $21,000.","Reports the valuation allowance as the net asset.","Ignores the valuation allowance.","Applies the unrealized percentage to the gross loss and omits the tax rate."],"wt":["","calc","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Net deferred tax asset equals the gross asset (loss times rate) less the valuation allowance.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gross DTA 250,000 x 21% = 52,500","Valuation allowance 52,500 x 60% = 31,500","Net DTA 52,500 - 31,500 = 21,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0024","t":"Harbor Textiles Inc. operates in two tax jurisdictions and files separate returns in each. In jurisdiction X, it has deferred tax assets of $80,000 and deferred tax liabilities of $130,000. In jurisdiction Y, it has deferred tax assets of $40,000 and deferred tax liabilities of $25,000. How are these presented on the classified balance sheet?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Textiles Inc. operates in two tax jurisdictions and files separate returns in each. In jurisdiction X, it has deferred tax assets of $80,000 and deferred tax liabilities of $130,000. In jurisdiction Y, it has deferred tax assets of $40,000 and deferred tax liabilities of $25,000. How are these presented on the classified balance sheet?","d":"","o":["A single noncurrent deferred tax liability of $35,000, netting all assets and liabilities together","Noncurrent deferred tax assets of $120,000 and noncurrent deferred tax liabilities of $155,000, presented gross","A noncurrent deferred tax liability of $50,000 and a noncurrent deferred tax asset of $15,000","A current deferred tax liability of $50,000 and a noncurrent deferred tax asset of $15,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-45-6; ASC 740-10-45-4","rat":"Deferred taxes are netted within each jurisdiction (X: $50,000 net liability; Y: $15,000 net asset) and classified as noncurrent.","ex":["Assets and liabilities of different tax jurisdictions cannot be offset against each other.","Offset is required within a jurisdiction, so the gross presentation overstates both.","Deferred taxes are netted within each jurisdiction (X: $50,000 net liability; Y: $15,000 net asset) and classified as noncurrent.","All deferred tax balances are classified as noncurrent."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deferred taxes offset only within a tax-paying component and jurisdiction, and are all classified as noncurrent.","from":"2026-01-01","steps":["Jurisdiction X: 80,000 - 130,000 = 50,000 net liability","Jurisdiction Y: 40,000 - 25,000 = 15,000 net asset","Both noncurrent, presented separately"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0025","t":"Slate Fabrication Inc. records book depreciation of $100,000 each year on a new asset. Tax depreciation is $160,000 in year 1 and $96,000 in year 2. The tax rate is 25% in both years. What is the deferred tax liability related to the asset at the end of year 2?","mc":{"s":"Slate Fabrication Inc. records book depreciation of $100,000 each year on a new asset. Tax depreciation is $160,000 in year 1 and $96,000 in year 2. The tax rate is 25% in both years. What is the deferred tax liability related to the asset at the end of year 2?","d":"","o":["$1,000","$14,000","$15,000","$16,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-30-8","rat":"Cumulative tax depreciation exceeds book by $60,000 - $4,000 = $56,000, and 25% of that is $14,000.","ex":["Uses only the year 2 difference, which is a reversal.","Cumulative tax depreciation exceeds book by $60,000 - $4,000 = $56,000, and 25% of that is $14,000.","Uses only the year 1 difference. The year 2 reversal of $4,000 reduces the cumulative difference.","Adds 8,000 to the cumulative difference, treating the reversal as an increase instead of a decrease."],"wt":["calc","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The deferred tax liability equals the rate times the cumulative excess of book carrying amount over tax basis at year-end.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Year 1 tax over book 160,000 - 100,000 = 60,000","Year 2 tax below book 96,000 - 100,000 = -4,000","Cumulative 56,000 x 25% = 14,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0026","t":"Copper Canyon Inc. has a taxable temporary difference that will reverse as follows: $200,000 in 2027 and $300,000 in 2028. Enacted law sets rates of 30% for 2027 and 25% for 2028. What is the deferred tax liability at December 31, 2026?","mc":{"s":"Copper Canyon Inc. has a taxable temporary difference that will reverse as follows: $200,000 in 2027 and $300,000 in 2028. Enacted law sets rates of 30% for 2027 and 25% for 2028. What is the deferred tax liability at December 31, 2026?","d":"","o":["$125,000","$135,000","$137,500","$150,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 740-10-30-8; ASC 740-10-55-23","rat":"Each portion is measured at the enacted rate in the year it reverses: $200,000 x 30% + $300,000 x 25% = $60,000 + $75,000 = $135,000.","ex":["Applies the lowest rate to the entire difference instead of the rate for each reversal year.","Each portion is measured at the enacted rate in the year it reverses: $200,000 x 30% + $300,000 x 25% = $60,000 + $75,000 = $135,000.","Uses a simple average of the two rates instead of scheduling the reversals.","Applies the 2027 rate to the entire difference."],"wt":["calc","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When different enacted rates apply to reversal years, measure each year's reversal at that year's rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["2027 reversal 200,000 x 30% = 60,000","2028 reversal 300,000 x 25% = 75,000","DTL 60,000 + 75,000 = 135,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0027","t":"Which of the following creates a taxable temporary difference and therefore a deferred tax liability?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following creates a taxable temporary difference and therefore a deferred tax liability?","d":"","o":["Estimated warranty costs accrued for books but deductible only when paid","Rent received in advance and taxed when received but recognized later for books","Equipment depreciated faster for tax than for financial reporting","Compensation expense accrued for books and deductible for tax only when paid to employees"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-20; ASC 740-10-20","rat":"Tax basis falls below book carrying amount, so future taxable amounts arise and a deferred tax liability results.","ex":["This creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset.","This creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset.","Tax basis falls below book carrying amount, so future taxable amounts arise and a deferred tax liability results.","This creates a deductible temporary difference and a deferred tax asset."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Book carrying amount above tax basis for an asset creates a taxable temporary difference and a deferred tax liability.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-D-0028","t":"Kestrel Media Inc. recorded a $500,000 goodwill impairment for financial reporting in 2026. The goodwill came from an acquisition of stock, and none of it is deductible for tax, now or in the future. The tax rate is 25%. What is the tax effect of the impairment?","mc":{"s":"Kestrel Media Inc. recorded a $500,000 goodwill impairment for financial reporting in 2026. The goodwill came from an acquisition of stock, and none of it is deductible for tax, now or in the future. The tax rate is 25%. What is the tax effect of the impairment?","d":"","o":["A deferred tax asset of $125,000 is recorded, because the impairment reduces book income","A deferred tax liability of $125,000 is recorded, because book goodwill has fallen below its tax basis","A current tax benefit of $125,000 is recorded, because the impairment is deductible in the current year","No deferred tax benefit is recorded, and the effective tax rate rises because the impairment is a permanent difference"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 740-10-25-3; ASC 805-740-25-9","rat":"Nondeductible goodwill creates no temporary difference, so the impairment reduces book income with no tax benefit and raises the effective tax rate.","ex":["A DTA requires a future tax deduction. Nondeductible goodwill produces none.","There is no tax basis in nondeductible goodwill, so no temporary difference or liability arises.","The impairment is not deductible for tax, so no current benefit arises.","Nondeductible goodwill creates no temporary difference, so the impairment reduces book income with no tax benefit and raises the effective tax rate."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Nondeductible goodwill has no tax basis; its impairment is a permanent difference that gives no deferred tax benefit.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"5":[{"i":"FAR-III-E-0001","t":"Which statement best describes how fair value is defined for financial reporting?","mc":{"s":"Which statement best describes how fair value is defined for financial reporting?","d":"","o":["The price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date","The price the entity paid to acquire the asset or the proceeds it received when it assumed the liability","The price the entity expects to receive by selling the asset in a forced liquidation within days","The amount the entity's management believes the asset is worth to its own operations"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 820-10-20; ASC 820-10-35-2","rat":"Fair value is an exit price, from the perspective of market participants, in an orderly transaction at the measurement date.","ex":["Fair value is an exit price, from the perspective of market participants, in an orderly transaction at the measurement date.","That describes an entry price. Fair value is an exit price.","A forced transaction is not an orderly transaction, so its price is not a fair value.","Entity-specific value in use is not the market participant view required for fair value."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fair value is an exit price: the amount received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly market transaction.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0002","t":"Which input is a Level 1 input in the fair value hierarchy?","mc":{"s":"Which input is a Level 1 input in the fair value hierarchy?","d":"","o":["A quoted price in an active market for an identical asset that the entity can access at the measurement date","A quoted price in an active market for a similar asset, adjusted for observable differences","An interest rate curve observable at commonly quoted intervals for the full term of the instrument, adjusted for credit spread","The entity's own projection of future cash flows from an asset used to build a valuation model"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-40; ASC 820-10-35-37","rat":"Level 1 inputs are unadjusted quoted prices in active markets for identical assets or liabilities.","ex":["Level 1 inputs are unadjusted quoted prices in active markets for identical assets or liabilities.","This is a Level 2 input. It is observable but not for an identical asset.","This is a Level 2 input, observable but not a quoted price for the identical asset.","Management's own projections are unobservable, Level 3, inputs."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Level 1 inputs are unadjusted quoted prices in active markets for identical items; Level 2 observable; Level 3 unobservable.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0003","t":"Larkin Commodities Inc. holds an asset that trades in its principal market at $50 per unit. Getting the asset to that market costs $3 per unit, and selling it there involves transaction costs of $2 per unit. Another market, in which Larkin does not usually transact, would pay $53 per unit, with $3 of transport and $2 of transaction costs. What is the fair value per unit?","mc":{"s":"Larkin Commodities Inc. holds an asset that trades in its principal market at $50 per unit. Getting the asset to that market costs $3 per unit, and selling it there involves transaction costs of $2 per unit. Another market, in which Larkin does not usually transact, would pay $53 per unit, with $3 of transport and $2 of transaction costs. What is the fair value per unit?","d":"","o":["$45","$47","$48","$50"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-5; ASC 820-10-35-9B","rat":"The principal market price of $50 is used, adjusted for transport costs of $3 because location is a characteristic of the asset.","ex":["Deducts transaction costs. Transaction costs are not part of fair value, though they are considered in choosing the most advantageous market.","The principal market price of $50 is used, adjusted for transport costs of $3 because location is a characteristic of the asset. Transaction costs are not deducted: $47.","Uses the most advantageous market. The principal market is used when one exists.","Ignores the transport cost. Costs to move the asset to its market adjust the price, because location is an asset characteristic."],"wt":["concept","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fair value uses the principal market price adjusted for transport costs, not for transaction costs.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Principal market price 50","Less transport costs 3 (location adjustment) = 47","Transaction costs of 2 are not deducted"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0004","t":"Ridgemont Properties Inc. owns land currently used as a parking lot, valued at $1,700,000 for that use. Zoning permits an office building, and market participants would pay $2,400,000 for the land to develop it, considering the cost of construction and expected returns. Ridgemont itself has no plans to develop. What is the fair value of the land?","mc":{"s":"Ridgemont Properties Inc. owns land currently used as a parking lot, valued at $1,700,000 for that use. Zoning permits an office building, and market participants would pay $2,400,000 for the land to develop it, considering the cost of construction and expected returns. Ridgemont itself has no plans to develop. What is the fair value of the land?","d":"","o":["$1,700,000, because the entity's current use of the asset determines its fair value","$2,050,000, the average of the current-use value and the development value","$2,400,000, because fair value assumes the highest and best use from the perspective of market participants","$1,700,000, because an entity may ignore a use it does not intend to pursue"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-10; ASC 820-10-35-10B","rat":"Highest and best use is legally permissible, physically possible and financially feasible from a market participant's viewpoint, even if the entity does not intend to use the asset that way.","ex":["The current use is presumed to be highest and best use only if market evidence does not suggest otherwise. Here market participants would pay more.","Fair value does not average uses. It reflects the use that maximizes value to market participants.","Highest and best use is legally permissible, physically possible and financially feasible from a market participant's viewpoint, even if the entity does not intend to use the asset that way.","The entity's intent is irrelevant. A market participant's perspective is used."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fair value of a nonfinancial asset assumes its highest and best use by market participants, whatever the entity plans.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0005","t":"Halcyon Fund Inc. holds 1,000,000 shares of an actively traded company. The quoted price is $20 per share, and selling the whole block at once would likely depress the price by 6%. What is the fair value of the holding?","mc":{"s":"Halcyon Fund Inc. holds 1,000,000 shares of an actively traded company. The quoted price is $20 per share, and selling the whole block at once would likely depress the price by 6%. What is the fair value of the holding?","d":"","o":["$10,000,000","$18,800,000","$20,000,000","$21,200,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-44; ASC 820-10-35-41","rat":"A Level 1 measurement is the quoted price times the quantity held, with no blockage discount: 1,000,000 x $20 = $20,000,000.","ex":["Applies an arbitrary liquidity discount with no basis in the measurement guidance.","Applies a blockage discount. A blockage factor is prohibited for Level 1 inputs.","A Level 1 measurement is the quoted price times the quantity held, with no blockage discount: 1,000,000 x $20 = $20,000,000.","Adds a premium of 6%, which reverses the direction of the adjustment and is also not allowed."],"wt":["trap","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A Level 1 measurement equals quoted price times quantity, without blockage adjustment.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Quoted price 20 x 1,000,000 shares = 20,000,000","No blockage factor for Level 1"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0006","t":"Meridian Wind Inc. estimates that market participants would expect an asset to produce net cash flows of $100,000 at the end of each of the next three years, with no residual value. The appropriate market discount rate is 10%. What is the fair value of the asset using the income approach?","mc":{"s":"Meridian Wind Inc. estimates that market participants would expect an asset to produce net cash flows of $100,000 at the end of each of the next three years, with no residual value. The appropriate market discount rate is 10%. What is the fair value of the asset using the income approach?","d":"","o":["$240,183","$248,685","$257,710","$300,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 820-10-55-3F; ASC 820-10-35-24","rat":"The present value of $100,000 for three years at 10% is $248,685.","ex":["Discounts at 12% instead of the 10% market rate.","The present value of $100,000 for three years at 10% is $248,685.","Discounts at 8% instead of the 10% market rate.","Adds the undiscounted cash flows, with no time value adjustment."],"wt":["calc","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The income approach converts expected future cash flows to a present value using a market participant discount rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["100,000 / 1.10 = 90,909","100,000 / 1.21 = 82,645; 100,000 / 1.331 = 75,131","Sum = 248,685"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0007","t":"Overton Endowment Inc. holds an investment in a private equity fund and measures it at the fund's reported net asset value per share, using the practical expedient. What is the reporting consequence for the fair value hierarchy disclosure?","mc":{"s":"Overton Endowment Inc. holds an investment in a private equity fund and measures it at the fund's reported net asset value per share, using the practical expedient. What is the reporting consequence for the fair value hierarchy disclosure?","d":"","o":["The investment is categorized as Level 1 because the fund reports a price per share","The investment is categorized as Level 2 because the NAV is derived from observable inputs","The investment is categorized as Level 3 because the fund's underlying assets cannot be observed","The investment is not categorized within the fair value hierarchy, but is reported separately to reconcile to the balance sheet"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-54B; ASC 820-10-50-2","rat":"Investments measured at NAV using the practical expedient are excluded from the hierarchy, with a separate reconciliation line.","ex":["A NAV per share for a private fund is not a quoted price in an active market.","Investments measured at NAV under the expedient are not categorized at any level.","Categorization is not required under the expedient. Level 3 applies to other measurements based on unobservable inputs.","Investments measured at NAV using the practical expedient are excluded from the hierarchy, with a separate reconciliation line."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Investments measured at NAV as a practical expedient are excluded from the fair value hierarchy categories.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0008","t":"Sterling Bancorp Inc. elected the fair value option for a note payable. During 2026, the fair value of the note decreased by $60,000, of which $45,000 resulted from a deterioration of the company's own credit standing, and the remainder from changes in market interest rates. How is the change reported?","mc":{"s":"Sterling Bancorp Inc. elected the fair value option for a note payable. During 2026, the fair value of the note decreased by $60,000, of which $45,000 resulted from a deterioration of the company's own credit standing, and the remainder from changes in market interest rates. How is the change reported?","d":"","o":["$60,000 gain entirely in net income","$60,000 gain entirely in other comprehensive income","$15,000 gain in other comprehensive income and $45,000 gain in net income","$15,000 gain in net income and $45,000 gain in other comprehensive income"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 825-10-45-5; ASC 825-10-35-5","rat":"For liabilities under the fair value option, the portion from instrument-specific credit risk goes to OCI ($45,000).","ex":["The instrument-specific credit risk portion must be presented in other comprehensive income.","Only the credit risk portion goes to OCI. The remaining change goes to net income.","Reverses the two components. Instrument-specific credit risk goes to OCI, and the rest goes to net income.","For liabilities under the fair value option, the portion from instrument-specific credit risk goes to OCI ($45,000). The remaining $15,000 goes to net income."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the fair value option for liabilities, the instrument-specific credit risk portion of a fair value change goes to OCI.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total decrease in fair value 60,000 (gain)","Instrument-specific credit risk 45,000 -> OCI","Remainder 60,000 - 45,000 = 15,000 -> net income"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0009","t":"Northfield Realty Inc. measures an office building using the price of a similar building sold recently, and adjusts it for differences in age, location and condition. The adjustments are based on management's own estimates and are significant to the total measurement. At which level is the measurement categorized?","mc":{"s":"Northfield Realty Inc. measures an office building using the price of a similar building sold recently, and adjusts it for differences in age, location and condition. The adjustments are based on management's own estimates and are significant to the total measurement. At which level is the measurement categorized?","d":"","o":["Level 2, because the price of a similar property is an observable input","Level 1, because the recent sale price is a quoted transaction price","Level 3, because a significant input is unobservable","The building is not categorized, because real estate is not a financial instrument"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-37A; ASC 820-10-35-52","rat":"A fair value measurement is categorized in its entirety by the lowest level input that is significant to the measurement.","ex":["The starting price is observable, but the significant unobservable adjustments determine the level.","Level 1 requires an unadjusted quoted price for an identical asset in an active market.","A fair value measurement is categorized in its entirety by the lowest level input that is significant to the measurement. A significant unobservable adjustment makes it Level 3.","Nonfinancial assets measured at fair value are categorized in the hierarchy too."],"wt":["trap","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A measurement is categorized by the lowest-level input significant to the whole measurement.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0010","t":"Cobalt Marine Inc. writes down a vessel to fair value at year-end after an impairment test. The fair value is determined by an independent appraisal that uses significant unobservable inputs. How is this measurement categorized and disclosed?","mc":{"s":"Cobalt Marine Inc. writes down a vessel to fair value at year-end after an impairment test. The fair value is determined by an independent appraisal that uses significant unobservable inputs. How is this measurement categorized and disclosed?","d":"","o":["As a nonrecurring Level 3 measurement, with disclosure of the fair value, the valuation technique and the significant unobservable inputs","As a recurring Level 2 measurement, because independent appraisals are observable evidence","As a recurring Level 3 measurement, because assets are remeasured to fair value every period","As not subject to fair value disclosures, because impairment losses are recognized in earnings"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 820-10-50-2; ASC 360-10-35-17","rat":"A one-time write-down measured at fair value is a nonrecurring measurement.","ex":["A one-time write-down measured at fair value is a nonrecurring measurement. Significant unobservable inputs place it in Level 3 with the related disclosures.","An impairment write-down measured only when triggered is nonrecurring. An appraisal with significant unobservable inputs is Level 3.","The vessel is not remeasured each period under GAAP, so the measurement is nonrecurring.","Assets written down to fair value are disclosed under the fair value guidance."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Assets measured at fair value only after an event such as impairment are nonrecurring measurements with hierarchy disclosures.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0011","t":"Delmont Utilities Inc. must measure the fair value of a note payable for disclosure. Which assumption is consistent with the fair value guidance?","mc":{"s":"Delmont Utilities Inc. must measure the fair value of a note payable for disclosure. Which assumption is consistent with the fair value guidance?","d":"","o":["The liability is settled with the creditor at its face amount, so credit risk is excluded","The liability is measured at the present value of payments using the risk-free rate only","The liability is measured by reference to the amount management expects to pay, without market inputs","The liability is transferred to a market participant who takes on the obligation, with the entity's own nonperformance risk reflected"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-16; ASC 820-10-35-17","rat":"Fair value of a liability assumes transfer, not settlement, and the effect of nonperformance risk, including the entity's own credit risk, is included.","ex":["Fair value assumes the liability is transferred and remains outstanding. It is not settled at face value.","The measurement reflects the entity's own nonperformance risk. A risk-free rate alone ignores it.","Fair value uses a market participant perspective, not management's expected payments alone.","Fair value of a liability assumes transfer, not settlement, and the effect of nonperformance risk, including the entity's own credit risk, is included."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A liability's fair value assumes transfer to a market participant and includes the entity's own nonperformance risk.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0012","t":"When might the transaction price paid to acquire an asset differ from its fair value at initial recognition?","mc":{"s":"When might the transaction price paid to acquire an asset differ from its fair value at initial recognition?","d":"","o":["Whenever the asset is acquired in a purchase of goods paid in cash and delivered in an ordinary transaction","Only when the buyer has negotiated a discount that is not available to other buyers","When the transaction is between related parties, or the seller is under duress or acting in a market different from the principal market","Whenever the price is paid in installments over more than one year"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 820-10-30-3A","rat":"Circumstances such as related parties, duress, or a different unit of account or market can make the transaction price differ from fair value.","ex":["In an ordinary arm's-length purchase, the transaction price is presumed to equal fair value.","Negotiated discounts alone do not make the price differ from fair value unless one of the recognized circumstances exists.","Circumstances such as related parties, duress, or a different unit of account or market can make the transaction price differ from fair value.","Payment terms are handled through time value adjustments, not by treating the price as unrelated to fair value."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Transaction price is presumed to equal fair value unless related parties, duress or a different market are involved.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0013","t":"Aster Timber Inc. has measured a nonpublic equity holding with a market approach based on comparable transactions. In 2026, comparable transactions stopped occurring, so management switches to an income approach. How is the change accounted for?","mc":{"s":"Aster Timber Inc. has measured a nonpublic equity holding with a market approach based on comparable transactions. In 2026, comparable transactions stopped occurring, so management switches to an income approach. How is the change accounted for?","d":"","o":["As a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively, with disclosure of the change and the reason","As a change in accounting principle applied retrospectively, with restated comparatives","As an error correction requiring restatement of the earlier periods' fair values","As a departure from GAAP that requires approval from the auditor before it is adopted"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 820-10-35-25; ASC 250-10-45-17","rat":"A change in valuation technique, or in its application, is treated as a change in accounting estimate, with disclosure of the change and the reason.","ex":["A change in valuation technique, or in its application, is treated as a change in accounting estimate, with disclosure of the change and the reason.","Valuation technique changes are treated as changes in estimate and are not applied retrospectively.","The prior measurements were appropriate when made. The change reflects new circumstances.","Changing a technique when circumstances warrant it is expected under the guidance. No approval is required."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A change in valuation technique is a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively with disclosure.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-E-0014","t":"Dunmore Fabrication Inc. measures a used machine using the cost approach. Current replacement cost of a new equivalent machine is $500,000. Physical deterioration is estimated at 20% of replacement cost, and functional obsolescence is estimated at $50,000. What is the fair value of the machine?","mc":{"s":"Dunmore Fabrication Inc. measures a used machine using the cost approach. Current replacement cost of a new equivalent machine is $500,000. Physical deterioration is estimated at 20% of replacement cost, and functional obsolescence is estimated at $50,000. What is the fair value of the machine?","d":"","o":["$350,000","$400,000","$450,000","$500,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 820-10-55-3D","rat":"Replacement cost less physical deterioration and functional obsolescence: $500,000 - $100,000 - $50,000 = $350,000.","ex":["Replacement cost less physical deterioration and functional obsolescence: $500,000 - $100,000 - $50,000 = $350,000.","Deducts only physical deterioration and ignores obsolescence.","Deducts only functional obsolescence and ignores physical deterioration.","Uses the replacement cost of a new machine without any deduction for deterioration or obsolescence."],"wt":["","calc","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The cost approach starts with current replacement cost and deducts physical, functional and economic obsolescence.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Replacement cost 500,000","Physical deterioration 500,000 x 20% = 100,000","Less functional obsolescence 50,000: 500,000 - 100,000 - 50,000 = 350,000"]}}],"6":[{"i":"FAR-III-F-0001","t":"Which fact, standing alone, causes a lease to be classified by the lessee as a finance lease?","mc":{"s":"Which fact, standing alone, causes a lease to be classified by the lessee as a finance lease?","d":"","o":["The lease term is 60% of the remaining economic life of the underlying asset","The present value of the lease payments is 85% of the fair value of the underlying asset","The lease term is 80% of the remaining economic life of the underlying asset, and the lease does not start near the end of that life","The lessee is responsible for paying property taxes and insurance on the asset"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-10-25-2","rat":"A lease term for the major part (75% or more) of the remaining economic life meets a finance lease criterion.","ex":["Sixty percent is below the 75% benchmark for major part of the economic life.","Eighty-five percent is below the 90% benchmark for substantially all of fair value.","A lease term for the major part (75% or more) of the remaining economic life meets a finance lease criterion.","Responsibility for executory costs is not a classification criterion."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A lease is a finance lease if it meets any one of five tests, including a term for 75% or more of remaining economic life.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0002","t":"Cedar Logistics Inc. leases equipment for five years, with annual payments of $40,000 due at the beginning of each year, starting at commencement. The discount rate is 8%. What is the initial lease liability? (Present value of an ordinary annuity for 4 periods at 8% is 3.31213, and for 5 periods is 3.99271.)","mc":{"s":"Cedar Logistics Inc. leases equipment for five years, with annual payments of $40,000 due at the beginning of each year, starting at commencement. The discount rate is 8%. What is the initial lease liability? (Present value of an ordinary annuity for 4 periods at 8% is 3.31213, and for 5 periods is 3.99271.)","d":"","o":["$132,485","$159,708","$172,485","$200,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-30-1; ASC 842-10-30-5","rat":"The first payment is made at commencement and is not discounted: $40,000 + $40,000 x 3.31213 = $172,485.","ex":["Includes only four payments discounted as an ordinary annuity, leaving out the payment made at commencement.","Treats the payments as an ordinary annuity, discounting the first payment for a full year.","The first payment is made at commencement and is not discounted: $40,000 + $40,000 x 3.31213 = $172,485.","Adds the undiscounted payments and ignores the time value of money."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The lease liability is the present value of remaining lease payments; a payment at commencement is not discounted.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Payment at commencement 40,000","Four remaining payments 40,000 x 3.31213 = 132,485","Lease liability 40,000 + 132,485 = 172,485"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0003","t":"Falcon Print Inc. signs a five-year lease with annual payments of $50,000 at the end of each year, and a discount rate of 6%. The lease liability is $210,618. Falcon also pays $8,000 of initial direct costs, prepays $5,000 of rent at commencement, and receives a $12,000 lease incentive from the lessor at commencement. What is the initial right-of-use asset?","mc":{"s":"Falcon Print Inc. signs a five-year lease with annual payments of $50,000 at the end of each year, and a discount rate of 6%. The lease liability is $210,618. Falcon also pays $8,000 of initial direct costs, prepays $5,000 of rent at commencement, and receives a $12,000 lease incentive from the lessor at commencement. What is the initial right-of-use asset?","d":"","o":["$210,618","$211,618","$223,618","$235,618"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 842-20-30-5; ASC 842-20-30-1","rat":"The right-of-use asset is the lease liability plus initial direct costs and prepaid payments, less incentives received: $210,618 + $8,000 + $5,000 - $12,000 = $211,618.","ex":["Uses only the lease liability. The asset also reflects direct costs, prepaid rent and incentives.","The right-of-use asset is the lease liability plus initial direct costs and prepaid payments, less incentives received: $210,618 + $8,000 + $5,000 - $12,000 = $211,618.","Ignores the lease incentive, which reduces the asset.","Adds the incentive instead of deducting it."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"ROU asset equals lease liability plus initial direct costs and prepayments, less lease incentives received.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Lease liability 210,618","Add initial direct costs 8,000 and prepaid rent 5,000 = 223,618","Deduct incentive 12,000 = 211,618"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0004","t":"Glenmore Air Inc. classifies a five-year lease as a finance lease. Annual payments are $50,000 at each year-end, the discount rate is 6%, and the initial lease liability and right-of-use asset are both $210,618. Ownership does not transfer. What is the total expense for year 1?","mc":{"s":"Glenmore Air Inc. classifies a five-year lease as a finance lease. Annual payments are $50,000 at each year-end, the discount rate is 6%, and the initial lease liability and right-of-use asset are both $210,618. Ownership does not transfer. What is the total expense for year 1?","d":"","o":["$12,637","$42,124","$50,000","$54,761"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 842-20-25-5; ASC 842-20-25-6","rat":"Interest is $210,618 x 6% = $12,637, and straight-line amortization is $210,618 / 5 = $42,124, totaling $54,761.","ex":["Reports interest only and omits the amortization of the asset.","Reports amortization only and omits interest on the liability.","Reports the cash payment. A finance lease expense is interest plus amortization.","Interest is $210,618 x 6% = $12,637, and straight-line amortization is $210,618 / 5 = $42,124, totaling $54,761."],"wt":["calc","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A finance lease produces interest on the liability plus amortization of the ROU asset, in place of a single lease cost.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Interest 210,618 x 6% = 12,637","Amortization 210,618 / 5 = 42,124","Total expense 12,637 + 42,124 = 54,761"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0005","t":"Using a finance lease with an initial liability of $210,618, annual year-end payments of $50,000 and a 6% rate, what is the lease liability immediately after the first payment?","mc":{"s":"Using a finance lease with an initial liability of $210,618, annual year-end payments of $50,000 and a 6% rate, what is the lease liability immediately after the first payment?","d":"","o":["$160,618","$168,495","$173,255","$223,255"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-35-1","rat":"The liability grows by interest of $12,637 and is reduced by the $50,000 payment: $210,618 + $12,637 - $50,000 = $173,255.","ex":["Subtracts the payment without adding the interest accrued during the year.","Reduces the liability by the amortization of the asset instead of the principal repaid.","The liability grows by interest of $12,637 and is reduced by the $50,000 payment: $210,618 + $12,637 - $50,000 = $173,255.","Adds interest but omits the payment made."],"wt":["calc","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The lease liability accretes interest at the discount rate and is reduced by each payment.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Opening liability 210,618","Interest 210,618 x 6% = 12,637","Less payment 50,000: 173,255"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0006","t":"Harborview Clinics Inc. enters into a five-year operating lease with annual payments of $30,000, $33,000, $36,000, $39,000 and $42,000, all paid at year-end. What is the single lease cost recognized in year 1?","mc":{"s":"Harborview Clinics Inc. enters into a five-year operating lease with annual payments of $30,000, $33,000, $36,000, $39,000 and $42,000, all paid at year-end. What is the single lease cost recognized in year 1?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$33,000","$36,000","$42,000"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-25-6","rat":"The total payments of $180,000 are recognized on a straight-line basis over the lease term: $36,000 per year.","ex":["Uses the year 1 cash payment. Operating lease cost is straight-line, not the cash paid.","Uses the second payment, with no connection to a straight-line pattern.","The total payments of $180,000 are recognized on a straight-line basis over the lease term: $36,000 per year.","Uses the final payment as the expense of every year."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An operating lease produces a single straight-line lease cost equal to total payments divided by the lease term.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total payments 30,000 + 33,000 + 36,000 + 39,000 + 42,000 = 180,000","Straight-line cost 180,000 / 5 = 36,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0007","t":"Ivy Consulting Inc. signs a four-year operating lease with level payments of $60,000 at each year-end and a 5% discount rate. The initial lease liability and right-of-use asset are $212,757. What is the right-of-use asset at the end of year 1?","mc":{"s":"Ivy Consulting Inc. signs a four-year operating lease with level payments of $60,000 at each year-end and a 5% discount rate. The initial lease liability and right-of-use asset are $212,757. What is the right-of-use asset at the end of year 1?","d":"","o":["$152,757","$159,568","$163,395","$223,395"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-35-3","rat":"Single lease cost is $60,000.","ex":["Reduces the asset by the full cash payment, ignoring the interest component.","Amortizes the asset straight-line over four years. For an operating lease, the asset reduction is the difference between the lease cost and interest on the liability.","Single lease cost is $60,000. Interest on the liability is $10,638, so the asset is reduced by the plug of $60,000 - $10,638 = $49,362: $212,757 - $49,362 = $163,395.","Increases the asset by interest on the liability, which affects the liability rather than the asset."],"wt":["calc","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For an operating lease, the ROU asset is reduced each period by straight-line cost less interest accreted on the liability.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Initial liability and ROU asset 60,000 x 3.54595 = 212,757","Interest year 1 212,757 x 5% = 10,638; single lease cost 60,000","ROU asset reduction 60,000 - 10,638 = 49,362; ending ROU asset 163,395"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0008","t":"Bluewater Marine Inc. leases a boat for ten months, with no purchase option and no option to extend. It elects the short-term lease exemption for this class of assets. How does it account for the lease?","mc":{"s":"Bluewater Marine Inc. leases a boat for ten months, with no purchase option and no option to extend. It elects the short-term lease exemption for this class of assets. How does it account for the lease?","d":"","o":["It recognizes lease payments as expense on a straight-line basis and does not record a right-of-use asset or lease liability","It records a right-of-use asset and lease liability, but does not discount the payments","It records the lease as a finance lease because the boat is used for most of its short life","It records nothing at all, and discloses the lease only in the notes to the financial statements"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-20-25-2","rat":"A short-term lease of twelve months or less, without a reasonably certain purchase option, can be expensed straight-line without balance sheet recognition.","ex":["A short-term lease of twelve months or less, without a reasonably certain purchase option, can be expensed straight-line without balance sheet recognition.","Under the exemption, neither an asset nor a liability is recorded.","The exemption applies regardless of classification, if the term is twelve months or less and no purchase option is reasonably certain.","The lease payments are recognized in the income statement as expense, and short-term lease cost is disclosed."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Leases of twelve months or less without a reasonably certain purchase option may be expensed straight-line without recognition on the balance sheet.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0009","t":"Kestrel Foods Inc. signs a five-year lease of a specialized packaging line. It has two options to renew, for three years each. Kestrel has made significant leasehold investments that would have no value after five years, and the first renewal rent is below market. Which is the lease term for measuring the lease liability?","mc":{"s":"Kestrel Foods Inc. signs a five-year lease of a specialized packaging line. It has two options to renew, for three years each. Kestrel has made significant leasehold investments that would have no value after five years, and the first renewal rent is below market. Which is the lease term for measuring the lease liability?","d":"","o":["Five years, because renewal options are excluded until they are exercised","Eleven years, because all options are included to the longest possible term","Three years, because the shortest renewal period defines the term","Eight years, because the first renewal is reasonably certain to be exercised given the economic incentive"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 842-10-30-1; ASC 842-10-55-26","rat":"The lease term includes optional periods when exercise is reasonably certain, considering economic factors such as significant leasehold improvements and below-market rents.","ex":["Options are included when exercise is reasonably certain, not only when exercised.","Only periods that are reasonably certain to be used are included. The second renewal has no similar incentive.","The noncancelable period is the base of the lease term, and renewals are added on top of it.","The lease term includes optional periods when exercise is reasonably certain, considering economic factors such as significant leasehold improvements and below-market rents."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Lease term is the noncancelable period plus renewal periods the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0010","t":"Amberley Dairy Inc. leases a truck for four years with payments of $25,000 at each year-end. Amberley guarantees the truck's residual value at $30,000 at the end of the lease. It expects the truck to be worth $22,000, so the expected amount owed under the guarantee is $8,000. The discount rate is 10%. What is the initial lease liability?","mc":{"s":"Amberley Dairy Inc. leases a truck for four years with payments of $25,000 at each year-end. Amberley guarantees the truck's residual value at $30,000 at the end of the lease. It expects the truck to be worth $22,000, so the expected amount owed under the guarantee is $8,000. The discount rate is 10%. What is the initial lease liability?","d":"","o":["$79,247","$84,711","$99,737","$108,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 842-10-30-5; ASC 842-20-30-1","rat":"The liability includes payments plus the amount probable of being owed under the residual value guarantee: $79,247 + $8,000 x 0.68301 = $84,711.","ex":["Excludes the expected guarantee payment. Amounts probable of being owed under a residual value guarantee are included.","The liability includes payments plus the amount probable of being owed under the residual value guarantee: $79,247 + $8,000 x 0.68301 = $84,711.","Includes the full $30,000 guarantee. Only the amount probable of being owed is included.","Adds the undiscounted payments and the expected guarantee payment."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Lease payments include amounts probable of being owed under a residual value guarantee, not the full guaranteed value.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["PV of payments 25,000 x 3.16987 = 79,247","Expected guarantee payment 30,000 - 22,000 = 8,000; PV 8,000 x 0.68301 = 5,464","Lease liability 79,247 + 5,464 = 84,711"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0011","t":"Portside Storage Inc. leases a warehouse with annual rent of $40,000 in year 1, and rent adjusts each year for changes in the consumer price index. At commencement, the lease liability is measured using the index at that date. In year 2, the index rises and rent is $41,600. How is the $1,600 increase accounted for?","mc":{"s":"Portside Storage Inc. leases a warehouse with annual rent of $40,000 in year 1, and rent adjusts each year for changes in the consumer price index. At commencement, the lease liability is measured using the index at that date. In year 2, the index rises and rent is $41,600. How is the $1,600 increase accounted for?","d":"","o":["As an increase in the lease liability and the right-of-use asset in year 2","As a reduction of the right-of-use asset with no effect on income","As a contingent liability that is disclosed but not recognized","As variable lease cost in year 2, without remeasuring the lease liability for the index change"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 842-10-30-5; ASC 842-20-25-5","rat":"Index-based increases after commencement are not reflected in the liability until remeasured for another reason, and the increase is expensed as incurred.","ex":["Changes in index-based payments do not by themselves trigger remeasurement of the liability in the year of the change.","The extra rent affects lease cost. It does not reduce the asset.","The rent is a period cost for use of the asset, and is recognized in income as a variable lease cost.","Index-based increases after commencement are not reflected in the liability until remeasured for another reason, and the increase is expensed as incurred."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Variable payments that depend on an index are measured at commencement; later changes are expensed as variable lease cost.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0012","t":"Boreal Machinery Inc. leases equipment that cost $380,000 under a sales-type lease. The present value of the lease payments is $470,000, and the present value of the unguaranteed residual value is $30,000. The fair value of the equipment is $500,000. What selling profit does Boreal recognize at commencement?","mc":{"s":"Boreal Machinery Inc. leases equipment that cost $380,000 under a sales-type lease. The present value of the lease payments is $470,000, and the present value of the unguaranteed residual value is $30,000. The fair value of the equipment is $500,000. What selling profit does Boreal recognize at commencement?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$120,000","$150,000","$470,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 842-30-25-1; ASC 842-30-30-1","rat":"Revenue is the lease receivable ($470,000) and cost of sales is the cost less the unguaranteed residual ($380,000 - $30,000): $470,000 - $350,000 = $120,000.","ex":["Ignores the deduction of the unguaranteed residual from cost of sales.","Revenue is the lease receivable ($470,000) and cost of sales is the cost less the unguaranteed residual ($380,000 - $30,000): $470,000 - $350,000 = $120,000.","Adds the residual to revenue and also deducts it from cost, double counting it.","Reports the lease receivable as profit."],"wt":["calc","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Sales-type lease profit is the receivable less cost of the asset net of the unguaranteed residual value.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Revenue: lease receivable 470,000","Cost of sales 380,000 - 30,000 = 350,000","Selling profit 470,000 - 350,000 = 120,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0013","t":"Everly Properties Inc. signs a 24-month operating lease with monthly rent of $5,000, with the first 3 months rent-free. Ignoring initial direct costs, how much lease income does Everly recognize in the first 12 months?","mc":{"s":"Everly Properties Inc. signs a 24-month operating lease with monthly rent of $5,000, with the first 3 months rent-free. Ignoring initial direct costs, how much lease income does Everly recognize in the first 12 months?","d":"","o":["$45,000","$52,500","$57,500","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 842-30-25-11","rat":"Total rent of $105,000 is recognized straight-line over 24 months: $4,375 per month x 12 = $52,500.","ex":["Recognizes only cash rent received in the first 12 months. Free rent is spread over the whole term.","Total rent of $105,000 is recognized straight-line over 24 months: $4,375 per month x 12 = $52,500.","Adds an extra month of rent to the straight-line amount.","Recognizes the full monthly rent for 12 months, ignoring the rent-free period."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A lessor recognizes operating lease income on a straight-line basis over the lease term, spreading free rent.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total rent (24 - 3) x 5,000 = 105,000","Monthly income 105,000 / 24 = 4,375","First 12 months 4,375 x 12 = 52,500"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0014","t":"Ashford Retail Inc. leases a store for ten years. In year 4, it agrees with the lessor to extend the term by three years at market rent, with no additional space added. The extension is not a separate contract because no additional right of use is granted. How does Ashford account for the modification?","mc":{"s":"Ashford Retail Inc. leases a store for ten years. In year 4, it agrees with the lessor to extend the term by three years at market rent, with no additional space added. The extension is not a separate contract because no additional right of use is granted. How does Ashford account for the modification?","d":"","o":["It keeps the original discount rate and adds the extension payments to the liability with no reassessment","It accounts for the extension as a new lease separate from the original, recognizing a second asset and liability","It remeasures the lease liability using the discount rate at the modification date, with a corresponding adjustment to the right-of-use asset","It recognizes the entire remeasurement as a gain or loss in the income statement in the year of modification"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-10-25-8; ASC 842-10-25-9","rat":"A modification that is not a separate contract requires reassessing classification and remeasuring the liability with an updated discount rate, adjusting the ROU asset.","ex":["The discount rate must be updated at the modification date, and classification is reassessed.","A separate contract arises only when the modification grants an additional right of use at a price consistent with standalone prices.","A modification that is not a separate contract requires reassessing classification and remeasuring the liability with an updated discount rate, adjusting the ROU asset.","The remeasurement adjusts the right-of-use asset. It is not recognized in income unless the asset is reduced to zero."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A lease modification that is not a separate contract remeasures the liability at an updated rate and adjusts the ROU asset.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0015","t":"Delmar Storage Inc. sells a warehouse, with a carrying amount of $800,000 and a fair value of $1,000,000, for $1,000,000 and leases it back for 14 years of the remaining 15-year economic life. The leaseback is a finance lease. How does Delmar account for the transaction?","mc":{"s":"Delmar Storage Inc. sells a warehouse, with a carrying amount of $800,000 and a fair value of $1,000,000, for $1,000,000 and leases it back for 14 years of the remaining 15-year economic life. The leaseback is a finance lease. How does Delmar account for the transaction?","d":"","o":["As a failed sale: it keeps the warehouse on its books and records a financial liability for the $1,000,000 proceeds","As a sale: it recognizes a $200,000 gain immediately and a finance lease for the leaseback","As a sale: it defers the $200,000 gain and recognizes it over the lease term","As an operating lease: it removes the warehouse and records the lease payments as rent expense"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-40-25-2; ASC 842-40-25-5","rat":"A leaseback classified as a finance lease means the buyer-lessor does not obtain control, so the transfer is not a sale and is accounted for as a financing.","ex":["A leaseback classified as a finance lease means the buyer-lessor does not obtain control, so the transfer is not a sale and is accounted for as a financing.","The sale criterion is not met when the leaseback is a finance lease, so no gain is recognized.","Deferral of gain applied under old guidance. Under current guidance, either the sale is recognized fully or it fails.","A finance lease classification means the leaseback cannot be treated as an operating lease."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If the leaseback is a finance lease, the transfer is not a sale and is accounted for as a financing.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0016","t":"Tamarack Textile Inc. sells a factory with a carrying amount of $900,000 for $1,300,000 and leases it back under an operating lease. The factory's fair value is $1,100,000. The transfer qualifies as a sale. What gain does Tamarack recognize?","mc":{"s":"Tamarack Textile Inc. sells a factory with a carrying amount of $900,000 for $1,300,000 and leases it back under an operating lease. The factory's fair value is $1,100,000. The transfer qualifies as a sale. What gain does Tamarack recognize?","d":"","o":["$200,000","$400,000","$1,100,000","$1,300,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-40-30-2; ASC 842-40-25-3","rat":"The sale price is adjusted to fair value, so the gain is $1,100,000 - $900,000 = $200,000.","ex":["The sale price is adjusted to fair value, so the gain is $1,100,000 - $900,000 = $200,000. The $200,000 excess price is treated as additional financing from the buyer-lessor.","Uses the stated price. Sales above fair value are adjusted, and the excess is a financing, not a gain.","Reports the fair value as the gain instead of the fair value less carrying amount.","Reports the sale price as the gain."],"wt":["","calc","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a sale-leaseback, price above fair value is additional financing; the gain equals fair value less carrying amount.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Adjust sale price to fair value 1,100,000; excess 200,000 is a financial liability","Gain 1,100,000 - 900,000 = 200,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0017","t":"Which rate does a lessee use to measure the lease liability at commencement?","mc":{"s":"Which rate does a lessee use to measure the lease liability at commencement?","d":"","o":["The lessee's weighted average cost of capital in all cases","The prime rate at the commencement date, for all lessees and all leases","The rate implicit in the lease if readily determinable; otherwise its incremental borrowing rate","The rate the lessor charges on its most recent lease, regardless of the lessee's own circumstances"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-30-3; ASC 842-20-30-4","rat":"The lessee uses the implicit rate when it can readily be determined.","ex":["Cost of capital is not the rate required for lease measurement.","The prime rate is not a required lease discount rate.","The lessee uses the implicit rate when it can readily be determined. Otherwise it uses its incremental borrowing rate for a similar term and collateral. A private company may elect a risk-free rate.","The lessor's other leases do not determine the lessee's implicit or incremental borrowing rate."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Use the rate implicit in the lease if readily determinable; otherwise the lessee's incremental borrowing rate.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0018","t":"Chandler Freight Inc. leases equipment for three years, paying $100,000 at each year-end. Of each payment, $20,000 is for maintenance services, a nonlease component. Chandler has not elected to combine lease and nonlease components, and the discount rate is 5%. What is the initial lease liability? (Present value of an ordinary annuity, 3 periods at 5%, is 2.72325.)","mc":{"s":"Chandler Freight Inc. leases equipment for three years, paying $100,000 at each year-end. Of each payment, $20,000 is for maintenance services, a nonlease component. Chandler has not elected to combine lease and nonlease components, and the discount rate is 5%. What is the initial lease liability? (Present value of an ordinary annuity, 3 periods at 5%, is 2.72325.)","d":"","o":["$217,860","$240,000","$272,325","$300,000"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-10-15-28; ASC 842-20-30-1","rat":"Only the lease component of $80,000 per year is included: $80,000 x 2.72325 = $217,860.","ex":["Only the lease component of $80,000 per year is included: $80,000 x 2.72325 = $217,860. Maintenance is accounted for separately as it is provided.","Uses the undiscounted lease component payments.","Includes the maintenance component. That is done only if the lessee elects to combine lease and nonlease components.","Uses undiscounted total payments including maintenance."],"wt":["","calc","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Nonlease components are excluded from the lease liability unless the lessee elects to combine them with the lease component.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Lease component per year 100,000 - 20,000 = 80,000","PV factor 3 periods at 5% = 2.72325","Lease liability 80,000 x 2.72325 = 217,860"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0019","t":"Grayson Tools Inc. has a finance lease and an operating lease. Under the indirect method, how are the cash payments for these leases reported in the statement of cash flows?","mc":{"s":"Grayson Tools Inc. has a finance lease and an operating lease. Under the indirect method, how are the cash payments for these leases reported in the statement of cash flows?","d":"","o":["Finance lease principal in financing, finance lease interest in operating, and operating lease payments in operating activities","All payments for both leases in financing activities, because a lease is a form of borrowing","Finance lease principal in investing activities, because it relates to acquiring a right-of-use asset","Operating lease payments in financing activities, and finance lease payments in operating activities"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-20-45-5","rat":"Principal payments on finance leases are financing outflows, while interest on finance leases and payments on operating leases are operating outflows.","ex":["Principal payments on finance leases are financing outflows, while interest on finance leases and payments on operating leases are operating outflows.","Operating lease payments and the interest portion of finance lease payments are operating activities.","Principal repayments of a finance lease liability are financing, not investing.","The classifications are reversed for both types."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Finance lease principal is financing; finance lease interest and operating lease payments are operating cash flows.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0020","t":"Maple Court Inc. installs leasehold improvements costing $300,000 with a useful life of 15 years. The lease term is 10 years, with one renewal option of 5 years that Maple is not reasonably certain to exercise, and ownership of the improvements reverts to the lessor at lease end. What is annual amortization?","mc":{"s":"Maple Court Inc. installs leasehold improvements costing $300,000 with a useful life of 15 years. The lease term is 10 years, with one renewal option of 5 years that Maple is not reasonably certain to exercise, and ownership of the improvements reverts to the lessor at lease end. What is annual amortization?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$30,000","$60,000","$300,000"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 842-20-35-12","rat":"Leasehold improvements are amortized over the shorter of their useful life and the lease term (including reasonably certain renewals): $300,000 / 10 = $30,000.","ex":["Uses the 15-year useful life, which exceeds the lease term. The improvements revert to the lessor at the end of the term.","Leasehold improvements are amortized over the shorter of their useful life and the lease term (including reasonably certain renewals): $300,000 / 10 = $30,000.","Uses the five-year renewal period only.","Expenses the full amount at installation. The improvements are capitalized and amortized."],"wt":["concept","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Leasehold improvements are amortized over the shorter of useful life and lease term, including reasonably certain renewals.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Useful life 15 years; lease term 10 years (renewal not reasonably certain)","Amortize over the shorter period, 10 years","300,000 / 10 = 30,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0021","t":"Sterling Optics Inc. leases equipment with a remaining economic life of 10 years for a 7-year term. Title does not transfer, there is no purchase option, and the equipment is not specialized. The present value of the lease payments is 92% of the equipment's fair value. How does Sterling classify the lease?","mc":{"s":"Sterling Optics Inc. leases equipment with a remaining economic life of 10 years for a 7-year term. Title does not transfer, there is no purchase option, and the equipment is not specialized. The present value of the lease payments is 92% of the equipment's fair value. How does Sterling classify the lease?","d":"","o":["As an operating lease, because the term is less than 75% of the remaining economic life and title does not transfer","As an operating lease, because title does not transfer and no purchase option exists","As a finance lease, because the term is more than 70% of the remaining economic life","As a finance lease, because the present value of the payments is 90% or more of fair value"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 842-10-25-2","rat":"The 7-year term is 70% of the life, below the 75% benchmark, but the present value test (90% or more) is met on its own.","ex":["Meeting one criterion is enough. The present value test is satisfied even though the term test is not.","Those two tests are not met, but the present value test alone is sufficient for a finance lease.","The correct threshold is 75%, and 70% does not meet it. The classification is right for a different reason.","The 7-year term is 70% of the life, below the 75% benchmark, but the present value test (90% or more) is met on its own."],"wt":["trap","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Meeting any one of five tests, such as payments valued at 90% or more of fair value, makes a lease a finance lease.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0022","t":"Greenbelt Leasing Inc. leases equipment with a carrying amount equal to its fair value. The lease does not transfer ownership or contain a purchase option, and the term is only 40% of the equipment's life, but a third party guarantees the residual value so that the present value of payments and the guarantee is 95% of fair value. How does Greenbelt classify the lease and what profit does it recognize at commencement?","mc":{"s":"Greenbelt Leasing Inc. leases equipment with a carrying amount equal to its fair value. The lease does not transfer ownership or contain a purchase option, and the term is only 40% of the equipment's life, but a third party guarantees the residual value so that the present value of payments and the guarantee is 95% of fair value. How does Greenbelt classify the lease and what profit does it recognize at commencement?","d":"","o":["A sales-type lease, with the difference between fair value and carrying amount recognized as profit","An operating lease, because the lease term is only 40% of the equipment's life","A direct financing lease, with a selling profit of 5% of fair value recognized at commencement","A direct financing lease, with no selling profit recognized at commencement"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 842-10-25-3; ASC 842-30-25-8","rat":"The lessor meets the present value test only because of the third-party guarantee, so it is a direct financing lease.","ex":["Because the third-party residual guarantee is needed to meet the test, the lease is a direct financing lease, and the carrying amount equals fair value in any case.","The present value test is met for the lessor when third-party guarantees are considered, so the lease is not an operating lease.","A direct financing lease is right, but there is no 5% selling profit at commencement. Selling profit on a direct financing lease is deferred into the net investment (only a selling loss would be recognized at commencement), and here carrying amount equals fair value anyway.","The lessor meets the present value test only because of the third-party guarantee, so it is a direct financing lease. Carrying amount equals fair value, so there is no selling profit at commencement."],"wt":["concept","trap","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A lease that meets only the third-party-guarantee present value test is a direct financing lease, without day-one selling profit.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0023","t":"Pinecrest Health Inc. leases a clinic for eight years with a renewal option for three years that it was not reasonably certain to exercise. In year 6, it builds a major addition that only makes economic sense if it uses the space for at least five more years. What is the accounting effect?","mc":{"s":"Pinecrest Health Inc. leases a clinic for eight years with a renewal option for three years that it was not reasonably certain to exercise. In year 6, it builds a major addition that only makes economic sense if it uses the space for at least five more years. What is the accounting effect?","d":"","o":["It continues to use the original term until the option is formally exercised in year 8","It adds the renewal payments to the liability at the original discount rate, without adjusting the asset","It reassesses the lease term, remeasures the lease liability using an updated discount rate, and adjusts the right-of-use asset","It recognizes a loss equal to the additional lease payments, since the option changes the lease cost"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-10-35-1; ASC 842-20-35-5","rat":"A significant event within the lessee's control, such as major leasehold improvements, that directly affects the option requires reassessment of the lease term and remeasurement.","ex":["Reassessment is required when a significant triggering event occurs, even before exercise.","Remeasurement uses an updated discount rate, and the right-of-use asset is adjusted for the change in liability.","A significant event within the lessee's control, such as major leasehold improvements, that directly affects the option requires reassessment of the lease term and remeasurement.","The change is reflected by remeasuring the liability and asset, not by an immediate loss."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A significant event within the lessee's control triggers reassessment of the term and remeasurement of the liability and ROU asset.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0024","t":"Aria Transport Inc. contracts with a supplier for the use of a truck for five years. The supplier may replace the truck with any similar truck at any time, and doing so costs the supplier very little. Aria directs how the trucks are used each day. Does the contract contain a lease?","mc":{"s":"Aria Transport Inc. contracts with a supplier for the use of a truck for five years. The supplier may replace the truck with any similar truck at any time, and doing so costs the supplier very little. Aria directs how the trucks are used each day. Does the contract contain a lease?","d":"","o":["No, because the supplier has a substantive substitution right that prevents the truck from being an identified asset","Yes, because Aria directs the use of the truck and receives all the benefits from it during the term","Yes, because the contract is for a period longer than twelve months","No, because trucks are movable equipment that cannot be identified as a specific asset"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-10-15-9; ASC 842-10-15-11","rat":"If the supplier has the practical ability to substitute the asset and would benefit economically, the asset is not identified and the contract is not a lease.","ex":["If the supplier has the practical ability to substitute the asset and would benefit economically, the asset is not identified and the contract is not a lease.","Control requires an identified asset. A substantive substitution right means there is no identified asset.","The contract term does not decide whether a lease exists.","Movable equipment can be identified. The reason there is no lease here is the substitution right."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A contract has a lease only if it conveys control of an identified asset; a substantive supplier substitution right defeats identification.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0025","t":"How does a lessee present its right-of-use assets and lease liabilities from operating and finance leases?","mc":{"s":"How does a lessee present its right-of-use assets and lease liabilities from operating and finance leases?","d":"","o":["Separately from other assets and liabilities, either on the face of the balance sheet or in the notes, with operating and finance leases distinguished","Combined with owned property, plant and equipment, since both are used in operations","Only finance leases are recorded on the balance sheet, while operating leases are disclosed in notes","As a net amount equal to the right-of-use asset less the lease liability, for each lease"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 842-20-45-1","rat":"The lessee presents right-of-use assets and lease liabilities separately from other items, and distinguishes finance from operating leases, on the balance sheet or in notes.","ex":["The lessee presents right-of-use assets and lease liabilities separately from other items, and distinguishes finance from operating leases, on the balance sheet or in notes.","Right-of-use assets are not combined with owned assets unless disclosed separately.","Operating leases longer than twelve months are also recognized on the balance sheet.","Assets and liabilities are not netted against each other."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Lessees present ROU assets and lease liabilities separately, distinguishing finance from operating leases.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-F-0026","t":"A finance lease has an initial liability of $210,618, annual year-end payments of $50,000 and a 6% rate. The liability after the first payment is $173,255. What is interest expense for year 2?","mc":{"s":"A finance lease has an initial liability of $210,618, annual year-end payments of $50,000 and a 6% rate. The liability after the first payment is $173,255. What is interest expense for year 2?","d":"","o":["$6,637","$10,110","$10,395","$12,637"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 842-20-35-1","rat":"Year 2 interest is the opening liability times the rate: $173,255 x 6% = $10,395.","ex":["Deducts two full payments from the initial liability, ignoring interest accretion.","Reduces the liability by straight-line amortization of the asset rather than the principal repaid.","Year 2 interest is the opening liability times the rate: $173,255 x 6% = $10,395.","Repeats the year 1 interest on the initial liability."],"wt":["calc","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Interest for each period equals the opening lease liability times the discount rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Opening liability year 2 = 173,255","Interest 173,255 x 6% = 10,395"]}}],"7":[{"i":"FAR-III-G-0001","t":"Which description correctly distinguishes recognized subsequent events from nonrecognized subsequent events?","mc":{"s":"Which description correctly distinguishes recognized subsequent events from nonrecognized subsequent events?","d":"","o":["Recognized events occur before the balance sheet date; nonrecognized events occur after the auditor's report date","Recognized events are those with a favorable effect; nonrecognized events are those with an unfavorable effect","Recognized events are recorded in the notes; nonrecognized events are recorded in the accounts","Recognized events provide evidence of conditions that existed at the balance sheet date; nonrecognized events arise after that date"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 855-10-25-1; ASC 855-10-25-3","rat":"Recognized events give additional evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date and adjust the statements.","ex":["The distinction depends on when the underlying condition arose, not on the auditor's report date.","The direction of the effect does not decide recognition.","The treatments are reversed. Recognized events adjust the accounts, and nonrecognized events are disclosed.","Recognized events give additional evidence about conditions existing at the balance sheet date and adjust the statements. Nonrecognized events concern conditions arising afterward and are disclosed if material."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Adjust for subsequent events that clarify conditions existing at the balance sheet date; disclose those that arise afterward.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0002","t":"At December 31, 2026, Ravenna Corp. accrued $300,000 for a lawsuit over an accident that occurred in 2026. In February 2027, before the 2026 financial statements were issued, the case was settled for $450,000. What adjustment does Ravenna make to its December 31, 2026 statements?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2026, Ravenna Corp. accrued $300,000 for a lawsuit over an accident that occurred in 2026. In February 2027, before the 2026 financial statements were issued, the case was settled for $450,000. What adjustment does Ravenna make to its December 31, 2026 statements?","d":"","o":["Make no adjustment and record the additional $150,000 in the 2027 statements","Increase the accrued liability and loss by $450,000","Increase the accrued liability and loss by $150,000","Disclose the settlement in the notes without adjusting the amounts"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 855-10-55-1; ASC 450-20-25-2","rat":"The settlement provides evidence about a condition that existed at year-end, so the estimate is updated to the $450,000 settlement: an additional $150,000 loss.","ex":["The accident occurred in 2026, so the settlement is a recognized event, and the 2026 statements are adjusted.","Records the full settlement without considering the $300,000 already accrued.","The settlement provides evidence about a condition that existed at year-end, so the estimate is updated to the $450,000 settlement: an additional $150,000 loss.","Disclosure alone applies to nonrecognized events. This settlement is a recognized event."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A settlement after year-end of a lawsuit for an event before year-end is a recognized subsequent event.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Settlement 450,000","Accrued at year-end 300,000","Adjust 2026: additional loss and liability 150,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0003","t":"At December 31, 2026, Harlan Industrial Inc. reported a $120,000 receivable from a customer whose finances had been deteriorating for months, with no allowance recorded for it. In January 2027, before the statements were issued, the customer filed for bankruptcy, and Harlan expects to collect only 10% of the balance. What loss should be reflected in the 2026 statements?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2026, Harlan Industrial Inc. reported a $120,000 receivable from a customer whose finances had been deteriorating for months, with no allowance recorded for it. In January 2027, before the statements were issued, the customer filed for bankruptcy, and Harlan expects to collect only 10% of the balance. What loss should be reflected in the 2026 statements?","d":"","o":["$120,000","$12,000","$0, with disclosure only, because the bankruptcy filing occurred after the balance sheet date","$108,000"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 855-10-55-1; ASC 326-20-30-1","rat":"The bankruptcy confirms a condition that existed at year-end, the customer's deteriorating finances, so it is a recognized event: $120,000 x 90% = $108,000.","ex":["Records the entire balance and ignores the expected 10% recovery.","Records only the expected recovery as the loss, reversing the collection percentage.","The filing confirms conditions that existed at year-end. It is not a new event, so adjustment is required.","The bankruptcy confirms a condition that existed at year-end, the customer's deteriorating finances, so it is a recognized event: $120,000 x 90% = $108,000."],"wt":["calc","calc","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A customer's bankruptcy after year-end caused by deterioration that existed at year-end is a recognized event.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deterioration existed at year-end: recognized event","Expected recovery 12,000; loss 120,000 - 12,000 = 108,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0004","t":"In January 2027, before the 2026 statements were issued, a fire destroyed Osprey Manufacturing Inc.'s main plant. The plant was insured only for part of its value. How should Osprey treat the fire in its December 31, 2026 financial statements?","mc":{"s":"In January 2027, before the 2026 statements were issued, a fire destroyed Osprey Manufacturing Inc.'s main plant. The plant was insured only for part of its value. How should Osprey treat the fire in its December 31, 2026 financial statements?","d":"","o":["Record the loss and write off the plant in the 2026 financial statements","Make no disclosure until the insurance claim has been settled in a later period","Disclose the nature of the event and an estimate of its financial effect, without adjusting the 2026 amounts","Restate 2026 depreciation to reflect the shortened life of the plant"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 855-10-25-3; ASC 855-10-50-2","rat":"The fire is a nonrecognized subsequent event because the condition arose after the balance sheet date.","ex":["The fire happened after year-end, so it does not adjust the 2026 statements.","Material nonrecognized events must be disclosed even if the final amount is not yet known.","The fire is a nonrecognized subsequent event because the condition arose after the balance sheet date. Disclosure of the nature and estimate is required.","The plant existed and was used throughout 2026, so no adjustment to prior depreciation is needed."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Events that arise after the balance sheet date, such as a fire, are nonrecognized and disclosed if material.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0005","t":"In February 2027, before its 2026 financial statements were issued, Quinault Energy Inc. issued $50 million of bonds to finance an expansion. How is the bond issuance reported in the 2026 statements?","mc":{"s":"In February 2027, before its 2026 financial statements were issued, Quinault Energy Inc. issued $50 million of bonds to finance an expansion. How is the bond issuance reported in the 2026 statements?","d":"","o":["It is a nonrecognized subsequent event, disclosed in the notes with its nature and effect","It is a recognized subsequent event, with a liability recorded at December 31, 2026","It is reported only as a pro forma reclassification of current debt to long-term debt","It requires no disclosure because financing decisions are within management's discretion and are not reportable events"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 855-10-55-2; ASC 855-10-50-2","rat":"The bond issuance occurred after year-end and did not give evidence about year-end conditions.","ex":["The bond issuance occurred after year-end and did not give evidence about year-end conditions. It is disclosed if material.","No obligation existed at year-end, so no liability is recorded.","A new issuance is not a reclassification of existing debt, and the balance sheet is not restated.","Material nonrecognized events must be disclosed."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A debt issuance after year-end is a nonrecognized subsequent event disclosed in the notes.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0006","t":"Two events occur after December 31, 2026, and before the statements are issued for Verona Apparel Inc. Event 1: inventory on hand at year-end is sold in January at a price below cost, because of a decline in fashion demand that began in the fourth quarter. Event 2: a flood in February destroys inventory held in a warehouse. Which describes the required treatment?","mc":{"s":"Two events occur after December 31, 2026, and before the statements are issued for Verona Apparel Inc. Event 1: inventory on hand at year-end is sold in January at a price below cost, because of a decline in fashion demand that began in the fourth quarter. Event 2: a flood in February destroys inventory held in a warehouse. Which describes the required treatment?","d":"","o":["Event 1 is a recognized event that adjusts year-end inventory; Event 2 is a nonrecognized event that is disclosed","Both events are recognized events that adjust the December 31, 2026 balance sheet","Event 1 is a nonrecognized event that is disclosed; Event 2 is a recognized event that adjusts inventory to its lower net realizable value","Both events are nonrecognized events that are disclosed, because both occurred after year-end"],"a":1,"cite":"ASC 855-10-25-1; ASC 855-10-25-3","rat":"The demand decline existed at year-end, so the January sales provide evidence of net realizable value.","ex":["The demand decline existed at year-end, so the January sales provide evidence of net realizable value. The flood arose after year-end and is disclosed.","The flood did not exist at year-end and so cannot adjust the December 31 balances.","The treatments are reversed. The January price decline reflects a year-end condition, while the flood is a new event.","The timing of the event does not decide. A subsequent event that clarifies a year-end condition is recognized."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Subsequent events that reveal conditions existing at year-end adjust the statements; new conditions are disclosed only.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0007","t":"Through what date must an entity evaluate subsequent events for its financial statements?","mc":{"s":"Through what date must an entity evaluate subsequent events for its financial statements?","d":"","o":["All entities evaluate through the balance sheet date only, because later events belong to the next period","All entities evaluate through the date the audit report is signed by the auditor, whether or not statements were issued","An SEC filer evaluates through the date the statements are issued; an entity that is not an SEC filer evaluates through the date they are available to be issued","An SEC filer evaluates through the date they are available to be issued; other entities through the date of issuance"],"a":3,"cite":"ASC 855-10-25-1A; ASC 855-10-50-1","rat":"SEC filers evaluate through issuance.","ex":["Subsequent events are events after the balance sheet date. Evaluation extends past it.","The evaluation period is defined by issuance or availability for issuance, not by the auditor's signature date.","SEC filers evaluate through issuance. Other entities evaluate through the date the statements are available to be issued and disclose that date.","The two categories are reversed."],"wt":["concept","trap","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"SEC filers evaluate subsequent events through the issuance date; other entities through the date available to be issued.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0008","t":"In January 2027, before issuing its 2026 statements, the board of Onyx Dynamics Inc. declared a two-for-one stock split. How are earnings per share for 2026 presented in the statements to be issued?","mc":{"s":"In January 2027, before issuing its 2026 statements, the board of Onyx Dynamics Inc. declared a two-for-one stock split. How are earnings per share for 2026 presented in the statements to be issued?","d":"","o":["Reported unchanged, because the split occurred after the balance sheet date, with no disclosure","Reported unchanged, with the effect of the split shown as a pro forma amount in the notes only","Restated by doubling net income, because the split doubles the number of shares","Restated retroactively for the split, with disclosure of the split as a subsequent event"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 260-10-55-12; ASC 855-10-55-2","rat":"A stock split after the balance sheet date but before issuance requires per-share data to be retroactively adjusted, and the split is disclosed.","ex":["Per-share amounts are retroactively adjusted for splits occurring before issuance.","The reported per-share amounts themselves must be restated, not only disclosed as pro forma.","A stock split does not change net income. It changes the number of shares and thus per-share amounts.","A stock split after the balance sheet date but before issuance requires per-share data to be retroactively adjusted, and the split is disclosed."],"wt":["concept","trap","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Stock splits after year-end but before issuance are retroactively reflected in per-share data.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0009","t":"At December 31, 2026, Solano Optics Inc. carried an obsolete product line at its cost of $500,000. The obsolescence resulted from a competitor's product released in November 2026. In February 2027, before the statements were issued, Solano sold the entire line for a net $420,000. What write-down should Solano record in the 2026 statements?","mc":{"s":"At December 31, 2026, Solano Optics Inc. carried an obsolete product line at its cost of $500,000. The obsolescence resulted from a competitor's product released in November 2026. In February 2027, before the statements were issued, Solano sold the entire line for a net $420,000. What write-down should Solano record in the 2026 statements?","d":"","o":["$500,000","$80,000","$420,000","None in 2026; the $80,000 loss is recognized in 2027 when the goods are sold"],"a":2,"cite":"ASC 855-10-55-1; ASC 330-10-35-1B","rat":"The February sale confirms the net realizable value of a year-end condition.","ex":["Writes off the entire cost. The inventory still has a net realizable value of $420,000.","The February sale confirms the net realizable value of a year-end condition. Inventory is written down $80,000 to $420,000.","Uses the sales proceeds as the write-down, not the difference between cost and proceeds.","The obsolescence existed at year-end, so the sale is a recognized event and the loss belongs to 2026."],"wt":["calc","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A post-year-end sale below cost that confirms a year-end condition is a recognized event requiring a write-down.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Obsolescence existed at year-end","Net realizable value from the February sale 420,000","Write-down 500,000 - 420,000 = 80,000"]}},{"i":"FAR-III-G-0010","t":"On February 10, 2027, the board of Talon Retail Inc. declared a $2 million cash dividend, before the 2026 financial statements were issued. How is the dividend reflected in the December 31, 2026 statements?","mc":{"s":"On February 10, 2027, the board of Talon Retail Inc. declared a $2 million cash dividend, before the 2026 financial statements were issued. How is the dividend reflected in the December 31, 2026 statements?","d":"","o":["It is recorded as a liability at December 31, 2026, with a reduction of retained earnings","It is recorded in the 2026 statements as an appropriation of retained earnings, without a liability","It is ignored in 2026 and disclosed only when the cash is paid in 2027","It is not recorded as a liability at year-end, and it is disclosed if material as a nonrecognized subsequent event"],"a":4,"cite":"ASC 855-10-55-2; ASC 855-10-50-2","rat":"A dividend liability arises on declaration.","ex":["No dividend liability existed at year-end, since the board had not yet declared the dividend.","Appropriating retained earnings for a dividend not declared by year-end is not required.","Disclosure is based on the declaration, not on the payment date.","A dividend liability arises on declaration. The declaration after year-end is a nonrecognized event, disclosed if material."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"III","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A dividend declared after year-end is not a liability at the balance sheet date and is disclosed if material.","from":"2026-01-01"}}]};
